WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.931 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.931 --> 00:00:03.839 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.839 --> 00:00:05.747 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.747 --> 00:00:10.517 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.517 --> 00:00:13.100 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So today we're going
to talk about Nash equilibrium.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.280 align:middle line:84%
So this is one of the
most important concepts

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.880 align:middle line:90%
in game theory.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.040 align:middle line:84%
But I want to start
by reminding you

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.250 align:middle line:84%
a little bit about
rationalizability

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.000 align:middle line:84%
because I think that's
going to provide us

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.260 align:middle line:84%
a good foundation for talking
about Nash equilibrium,

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.800 align:middle line:84%
seeing how these
two concepts differ.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.280 align:middle line:84%
So as before and
throughout this class,

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.980 align:middle line:84%
we're going to be given
a strategic form game.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.900 align:middle line:84%
And this notation is
going to stay the same.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.300 align:middle line:84%
So we have a
strategic form game.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:45.020 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll write
strat-form game,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:51.280 align:middle line:84%
which specifies the set
of strategies, the n

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.840 align:middle line:84%
players, and then the
payoffs that each player gets

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.440 align:middle line:84%
as a function of the strategy
profiles that are chosen.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.940 align:middle line:84%
And last time, we
defined this procedure,

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.260 align:middle line:84%
which we called iterated
elimination of strictly

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.780 align:middle line:90%
dominated strategies.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.140 align:middle line:84%
And this was an
iterative algorithm

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.300 align:middle line:84%
that gradually narrowed down
or pared down the strategies

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.640 align:middle line:90%
that the players had.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.900 align:middle line:84%
So we started with, if you
remember, the first stage

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.220 align:middle line:84%
or stage 0, the
initialization stage

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.580 align:middle line:84%
was just the set
of all strategies,

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.060 align:middle line:90%
all strategy profiles.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:33.380 align:middle line:84%
And then we went
to big S, S1, S2,

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:39.060 align:middle line:84%
and we kept going down until
maybe we had a generic SK,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.620 align:middle line:84%
and then, at the end, we had
something called S infinity.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.260 align:middle line:84%
And it's important
to understand--

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.220 align:middle line:90%
maybe I'll write this up here--

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.300 align:middle line:84%
the interpretation of
each of these sets.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.160 align:middle line:84%
So below, let me say what
is the interpretation?

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.540 align:middle line:90%


00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.040 align:middle line:84%
The interpretation
is that, as we're

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.640 align:middle line:84%
willing to make stronger and
stronger behavioral assumptions

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.480 align:middle line:84%
about the way that
the players play,

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.240 align:middle line:84%
we're able to make sharper
and sharper predictions

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.680 align:middle line:84%
about the strategies that we
could observe in practice.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.920 align:middle line:90%
So we start with S0.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.420 align:middle line:90%
This imposed no assumptions.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.520 align:middle line:90%


00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.240 align:middle line:84%
If we make no assumptions
about how people play, then,

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:24.920 align:middle line:90%
of course, anything is possible.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.040 align:middle line:84%
The set of strategy profiles
that the players may choose

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.640 align:middle line:84%
is unrestricted, but
then we move to S1,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.240 align:middle line:84%
where, there, the
interpretation is rationality.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.960 align:middle line:84%
What we said is, what if we
assume that every player is

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.360 align:middle line:84%
rational in the sense that
every player plays a best

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.080 align:middle line:84%
response to some belief
about the opposing player's

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.480 align:middle line:90%
strategies?

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.497 align:middle line:84%
And if we assume that
every player is rational,

00:02:49.497 --> 00:02:51.080 align:middle line:84%
then we can narrow
down our prediction

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.200 align:middle line:90%
in the game to the set S1.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.620 align:middle line:84%
So capital S1 is the
set of strategy profiles

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.700 align:middle line:84%
that are consistent with
every player's rationality.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.500 align:middle line:84%
And just to remember-- maybe
I'll put it down here--

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:08.020 align:middle line:84%
when I write S capital K,
this is really a product.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.100 align:middle line:90%
Like this.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:12.780 align:middle line:84%
So S capital K is a set
of strategy profiles

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.620 align:middle line:84%
that contain a strategy for
player 1, in this set S1 K,

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.260 align:middle line:84%
all the way up to a strategy
for player n in the set SN K.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.400 align:middle line:84%
And then we could go
beyond just rationality.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.340 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we went
to S2, and there, we

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.600 align:middle line:84%
assumed not only that
the players are rational

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.600 align:middle line:84%
but that the players know that
everyone else is rational.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:38.100 align:middle line:84%
So we might call this
rationality plus knowledge

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.180 align:middle line:90%
of rationality.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:43.500 align:middle line:84%
I'll just write knowledge
of R, so rationality

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.522 align:middle line:90%
plus knowledge of rationality.

00:03:46.522 --> 00:03:47.980 align:middle line:84%
But then, as we
keep going down, we

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.020 align:middle line:84%
can get to higher- and
higher-order knowledge

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.080 align:middle line:90%
of rationality.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.920 align:middle line:84%
So when we get to SK, the
interpretation of this is SK is

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.200 align:middle line:84%
our prediction in the game
if we're willing to assume

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.360 align:middle line:84%
rationality, knowledge
of rationality,

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.890 align:middle line:84%
knowledge of
others' rationality,

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.140 align:middle line:84%
knowledge of others' knowledge
of others' rationality,

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:09.240 align:middle line:84%
and so on, all the way down to
maybe I'll say rationality plus

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.860 align:middle line:84%
"K minus 1 order"
knowledge of rationality.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:19.200 align:middle line:90%


00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.560 align:middle line:90%
Why K minus 1?

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.100 align:middle line:84%
Well, the first stage,
we had rationality

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.760 align:middle line:84%
but no knowledge of rationality,
so the degree of knowledge

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.200 align:middle line:84%
of rationality is always just
1 smaller than our number K.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.880 align:middle line:84%
And when we get up to K, this
means the players are rational.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.380 align:middle line:84%
They know everyone
else is rational,

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.755 align:middle line:84%
they know that everyone else
knows that they're rational,

00:04:36.755 --> 00:04:37.420 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.240 align:middle line:84%
And those statements
go on until I say

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.553 align:middle line:90%
the word knows k minus 1 times.

00:04:42.553 --> 00:04:44.220 align:middle line:84%
And then if we want
to be really formal,

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.240 align:middle line:84%
there's a word
for this last set.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.320 align:middle line:84%
So this last set,
these are the set

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.220 align:middle line:90%
of rationalizable strategies.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:55.060 align:middle line:90%


00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.540 align:middle line:84%
And the interpretation
of this is

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.900 align:middle line:84%
that these are the
strategy profiles that

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.660 align:middle line:84%
are consistent with
rationality and arbitrarily

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.980 align:middle line:84%
high degrees of
knowledge of rationality.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.900 align:middle line:84%
So not only does each
player everyone else

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.460 align:middle line:84%
knows their rational, but
I can continue those chains

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.980 align:middle line:90%
arbitrarily deep.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:11.580 align:middle line:84%
And we have a word
for that-- that's

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.780 align:middle line:84%
called common knowledge
of rationality.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:27.860 align:middle line:84%
So I'll write-- just write R. So
rationalizable captures the idea

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.720 align:middle line:84%
that not only are
the players rational,

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.460 align:middle line:84%
but they have common knowledge
of rationality in the sense that

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.940 align:middle line:84%
they have K-th order knowledge
of rationality for any K.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.160 align:middle line:84%
They have first order,
second order, third order,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.700 align:middle line:84%
fourth order, 100th
order, and so on.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.580 align:middle line:84%
So this is kind of a summary
of where we've gone so far.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.090 align:middle line:84%
And I want you to notice how, as
we continue to the right here,

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:52.730 align:middle line:84%
we make stronger and stronger
behavioral assumptions,

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:54.890 align:middle line:84%
and we get sharper and
sharper predictions.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.450 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes, even
this set S infinity

00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:59.550 align:middle line:84%
is not going to give us
a very sharp prediction.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:00.990 align:middle line:84%
And we want to go
a step further.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.530 align:middle line:84%
And that's what Nash equilibrium
is going to do today.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:06.530 align:middle line:84%
So to motivate that,
let's start with

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:10.970 align:middle line:84%
or revisit an example that
we've done before, which

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:13.190 align:middle line:90%
we call the Boston game, BOS.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:14.690 align:middle line:84%
And remember, the
story of this game

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:16.930 align:middle line:84%
is we had two friends who
like each other's company

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:18.550 align:middle line:84%
but have different
sports preferences.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:21.170 align:middle line:84%
So they're choosing whether to
go to a Celtics game or a Red

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:22.070 align:middle line:90%
Sox game.

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.550 align:middle line:84%
They're both happier if
they go together than apart,

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.130 align:middle line:84%
but one person prefers
the Celtics and one person

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:28.490 align:middle line:90%
prefers the Red Sox.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:32.322 align:middle line:84%
So if we write out
the game like this--

00:06:32.322 --> 00:06:34.530 align:middle line:84%
I think I'll use slightly
different numbers this time

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:37.670 align:middle line:84%
to make some of the
math later a bit easier.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.850 align:middle line:90%


00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.090 align:middle line:90%
So let's go over it.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:45.250 align:middle line:84%
If we choose differently,
that's along this off diagonal,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.710 align:middle line:84%
then we both get a payoff
of 0 because we don't

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.710 align:middle line:90%
get to be together at the game.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.690 align:middle line:84%
If we both go to
the Celtics game,

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:54.470 align:middle line:84%
then we both get
a positive payoff.

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:56.870 align:middle line:84%
But it's higher for
player 1 because player 1

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:58.510 align:middle line:90%
likes the Celtics more.

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:01.810 align:middle line:84%
And then conversely, if we
both go to the Red Sox game,

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:03.410 align:middle line:90%
we both get a positive payout.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:06.070 align:middle line:84%
But here, it's the second
player who prefers the Red Sox

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:09.150 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, gets
the higher payoff of 2.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.950 align:middle line:84%
So let's go through
our procedure

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:14.830 align:middle line:84%
for calculating
best responses here.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.130 align:middle line:84%
If my opponent is going
to the Celtics game,

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:18.922 align:middle line:84%
I certainly want to go
to the Celtics game.

00:07:18.922 --> 00:07:20.630 align:middle line:84%
And if they're going
to the Red Sox game,

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:22.090 align:middle line:84%
I want to go to
the Red Sox game.

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:23.590 align:middle line:84%
And then we can do
it the other way.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:25.870 align:middle line:84%
Now, I'll put myself in
the shoes of player 2.

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:29.870 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying, if player 1 plays
C, well, I look at my payoffs.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:33.230 align:middle line:84%
I prefer C. If player one
plays R, I look at my payoffs,

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:35.230 align:middle line:90%
I prefer this.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:38.110 align:middle line:84%
So now, if we look
at that, can anyone

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:41.390 align:middle line:84%
see, what are the set of
rationalizable strategies

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:43.610 align:middle line:84%
or strategy profiles
in this game?

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:48.810 align:middle line:84%
So maybe we'll separate
it out just to be clear.

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.050 align:middle line:84%
So what are the set of
rationalizable strategies

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:52.710 align:middle line:90%
for player 1 in this game?

00:07:52.710 --> 00:08:02.290 align:middle line:90%


00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:04.250 align:middle line:84%
Well, here, it's
going to be just C

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:09.330 align:middle line:84%
And R because, here,
if my opponent plays C,

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:13.512 align:middle line:84%
I want to play C. If my opponent
plays R, I want to play R.

00:08:13.512 --> 00:08:14.970 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out
everything is going

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:17.290 align:middle line:84%
to be rationalizable
in this game.

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:18.850 align:middle line:90%
So we're going to get--

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:22.050 align:middle line:84%
if we go through
the procedure C, R,

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:26.970 align:middle line:84%
and if we go through the
procedure, we also get C, R.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:29.090 align:middle line:84%
And just to make sure we
understand the notation,

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:32.710 align:middle line:84%
S infinity is the set of
rationalizable profiles.

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:34.669 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to
have four elements.

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:41.770 align:middle line:84%
It's going to have
CC, CR, RC, and RR.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:43.370 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes I'll write
with parentheses,

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:45.150 align:middle line:84%
but it just gets a
little messy here.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.870 align:middle line:84%
So this says there's
four strategy profiles.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:51.190 align:middle line:84%
This is the profile where we
both go to the Celtics game.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:54.070 align:middle line:84%
This is the profile where we
both go to the Red Sox game.

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.170 align:middle line:84%
And the order here
indicates who does what.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:59.350 align:middle line:84%
So the first player plays C,
the second player plays R,

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:01.630 align:middle line:84%
and then, conversely,
the first player plays R

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:03.830 align:middle line:90%
and the second player plays C.

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:07.310 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, everything
is rationalizable.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:11.150 align:middle line:84%
So this is a case where
rationality and common knowledge

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:14.710 align:middle line:84%
of rationality alone doesn't
give us a very good prediction

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:16.908 align:middle line:84%
or doesn't rule out
many possibilities.

00:09:16.908 --> 00:09:18.950 align:middle line:84%
So we're not able to get
a very sharp prediction.

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:22.430 align:middle line:84%
And let's see, so in particular,
one rationalizable strategy,

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:25.750 align:middle line:90%
profile, I should say, is this.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:29.150 align:middle line:90%
So this is rationalizable.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:32.110 align:middle line:84%
But it may seem like
something is going wrong here.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.607 align:middle line:84%
So does this seem like
a reasonable prediction

00:09:34.607 --> 00:09:35.690 align:middle line:90%
of how people are playing?

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:39.430 align:middle line:84%
Or would you say someone's
making a mistake here?

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:41.670 align:middle line:84%
What seems strange
about this-- when

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:45.130 align:middle line:84%
player 1 is going to the
Red Sox and player 2 is

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:48.490 align:middle line:90%
going to the Celtics game?

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:48.990 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.070 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
since they're both losing.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:52.410 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: They're both losing.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.310 align:middle line:84%
And in a sense, well, I'm
going to the Red Sox game,

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:56.970 align:middle line:84%
but if I just
changed my behavior

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.083 align:middle line:84%
and went to the Celtics
game, I'd be better off.

00:09:59.083 --> 00:10:01.750 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, the other person
who's alone at the Celtics game,

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:04.042 align:middle line:84%
if they move to the Red Sox
game, they'd be better off.

00:10:04.042 --> 00:10:07.890 align:middle line:84%
So there's a bit
of this incongruity

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:09.490 align:middle line:84%
that we say, OK,
this is consistent

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:12.173 align:middle line:84%
with rationality and common
knowledge of rationality,

00:10:12.173 --> 00:10:14.090 align:middle line:84%
but it might feel somehow
that the players are

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:15.490 align:middle line:90%
making a mistake.

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:19.230 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll say this
is all a bit informal.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:20.430 align:middle line:90%
It feels like a mistake.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:25.370 align:middle line:90%


00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:26.790 align:middle line:90%
And let's try to reconcile this.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:29.890 align:middle line:84%
Why does this satisfy the
definition of rationalizability

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:31.310 align:middle line:90%
but feel like a mistake?

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:32.685 align:middle line:84%
And I think the
best way to think

00:10:32.685 --> 00:10:35.730 align:middle line:84%
about that is, what is
rationalizability really mean?

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:37.330 align:middle line:84%
It really means
that a player has

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:40.430 align:middle line:84%
an explanation for why they're
doing what they're doing.

00:10:40.430 --> 00:10:42.030 align:middle line:84%
So I think
rationalizability should

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:48.730 align:middle line:84%
be thought of as it means that
a player has an explanation.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:51.910 align:middle line:90%


00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:54.310 align:middle line:84%
So what is player
1's explanation

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:56.060 align:middle line:84%
for why they're going
to the Red Sox game?

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:58.518 align:middle line:84%
They would say, well, it's
reasonable, it's rational for me

00:10:58.518 --> 00:10:59.990 align:middle line:84%
to go to the Red
Sox game because I

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:04.195 align:middle line:84%
believe that my friend is also
going to the Red Sox game.

00:11:04.195 --> 00:11:05.570 align:middle line:84%
And then we can
go a step deeper.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.430 align:middle line:84%
We could say, well, why do you
believe that your friend is

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:09.610 align:middle line:90%
going to the Red Sox game?

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:10.590 align:middle line:84%
And you could say,
well, I believe

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:12.330 align:middle line:84%
my friend is going
to the Red Sox game

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.413 align:middle line:84%
because I believe that my
friend believes that I'm

00:11:14.413 --> 00:11:15.590 align:middle line:90%
going to the Red Sox game.

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:16.650 align:middle line:90%
And then you go a step deeper.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:18.750 align:middle line:84%
You can say, why do you believe
that your friend believes

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:19.970 align:middle line:84%
that you're going
to the Red Sox game?

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:22.270 align:middle line:84%
Well, I think my friend
believes that I believe

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.130 align:middle line:84%
that my friend believes he's
going to the Red Sox game,

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:27.770 align:middle line:84%
and that explains his belief,
and we can go on and on and on.

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:30.450 align:middle line:84%
And that's precisely what
rationalizability captures.

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:32.550 align:middle line:84%
It says you can provide
an explanation that

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:33.678 align:middle line:90%
goes arbitrarily deep.

00:11:33.678 --> 00:11:35.470 align:middle line:84%
So think of this as
the answer to the child

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:36.877 align:middle line:90%
who always says, but why this?

00:11:36.877 --> 00:11:38.210 align:middle line:90%
You can always go a step deeper.

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:39.310 align:middle line:90%
Why do they have this belief?

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:41.685 align:middle line:84%
Well, they have this belief
because of this other belief.

00:11:41.685 --> 00:11:43.210 align:middle line:90%
And that chain never stops.

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:45.403 align:middle line:84%
If it weren't rationalizable,
eventually, we'd

00:11:45.403 --> 00:11:46.070 align:middle line:90%
reach a problem.

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:47.890 align:middle line:84%
We'd reach a contradiction
in that chain.

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:50.690 align:middle line:84%
So rationalizability says,
there exists some explanation

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:51.770 align:middle line:90%
for your play.

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:54.250 align:middle line:84%
The problem is your
explanation may not be correct.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:56.270 align:middle line:84%
In theory, it's a
good explanation,

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:58.410 align:middle line:84%
but it might not actually
comport with the way

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.330 align:middle line:90%
that people are playing.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:03.230 align:middle line:84%
So rationalizability means
you have an explanation.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:05.490 align:middle line:84%
You're behaving optimally,
given this explanation

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:08.050 align:middle line:84%
or given these beliefs,
but there's no requirement

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:18.050 align:middle line:84%
that the explanation is
correct, that your beliefs

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:19.230 align:middle line:90%
or your explanation--

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:23.030 align:middle line:84%
I'll use these
explanations a bit deeper.

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:28.690 align:middle line:90%


00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:32.370 align:middle line:84%
Because if we're down here, my
explanation involved my belief

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:37.010 align:middle line:84%
that my friend was also going to
the Red Sox game, but in fact,

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:38.210 align:middle line:90%
my friend is not doing that.

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:39.670 align:middle line:84%
My friend is going
to the Celtics game,

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:41.070 align:middle line:90%
and that's why I'm doing badly.

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:43.390 align:middle line:84%
So what Nash equilibrium
is going to capture

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:45.430 align:middle line:84%
is it's going to go
a step beyond simply

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:47.150 align:middle line:90%
providing an explanation.

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:49.590 align:middle line:84%
It's going to require that
the explanations we provide

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:50.730 align:middle line:90%
are also correct.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:52.790 align:middle line:84%
That is, they also
accurately describe the way

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:54.665 align:middle line:84%
that our opponents or
that our fellow players

00:12:54.665 --> 00:12:56.990 align:middle line:90%
are actually playing.

00:12:56.990 --> 00:13:01.670 align:middle line:84%
So let's now give the
formal definition of this.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.130 align:middle line:84%
So if you learn one
thing about game theory,

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:08.033 align:middle line:84%
this is often the
definition you see.

00:13:08.033 --> 00:13:09.950 align:middle line:84%
In the movie about Nash,
they talk about this.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:12.450 align:middle line:84%
Though, actually, their story
is kind of wrong in the movie.

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:15.050 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't make
mathematical sense.

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:16.030 align:middle line:90%
But anyway.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:18.530 align:middle line:90%
OK, so a strategy profile--

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:27.887 align:middle line:90%


00:13:27.887 --> 00:13:29.470 align:middle line:84%
let me just stop
here and say, I think

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:31.678 align:middle line:84%
this is really an important
aspect of the definition,

00:13:31.678 --> 00:13:35.090 align:middle line:84%
that the thing that a
Nash equilibrium will be

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:36.690 align:middle line:90%
is a strategy profile.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:38.208 align:middle line:84%
And often, a mistake
students make

00:13:38.208 --> 00:13:40.250 align:middle line:84%
on exams is they're asked
to give an equilibrium,

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:42.517 align:middle line:84%
and they often don't
give a strategy profile.

00:13:42.517 --> 00:13:44.850 align:middle line:84%
They might give the payoffs
that the players get if they

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:46.230 align:middle line:90%
use that strategy profile.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:48.690 align:middle line:84%
But an equilibrium is
always a strategy profile

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:52.022 align:middle line:84%
because it's a prediction
or recommendation for how

00:13:52.022 --> 00:13:53.230 align:middle line:90%
people will play in the game.

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:54.950 align:middle line:90%
So this is the key first part.

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:57.210 align:middle line:84%
It's a profile
specifying a strategy

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:58.810 align:middle line:84%
for the first
player, all the way

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:00.970 align:middle line:84%
up to a strategy
for the n-th player.

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:04.670 align:middle line:84%
So this kind of strategy
profile is a Nash equilibrium.

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:09.850 align:middle line:90%


00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:14.210 align:middle line:84%
So this is named after
John Nash who won the Nobel

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:15.030 align:middle line:90%
Prize in economics.

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:16.990 align:middle line:90%
He wrote this paper in '48, '49.

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:19.290 align:middle line:84%
He wrote a few papers that
introduced this concept.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:21.590 align:middle line:84%
It's actually interesting
to read the original paper.

00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:23.813 align:middle line:84%
The original paper is
like two paragraphs long,

00:14:23.813 --> 00:14:25.230 align:middle line:84%
and he got the
Nobel Prize for it.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:27.090 align:middle line:84%
So if you have good
ideas, you don't

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:28.430 align:middle line:90%
need to go on and on and on.

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:31.010 align:middle line:84%
So a strategy profile
is a Nash equilibrium

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:41.310 align:middle line:84%
if, for every player
i, the following holds.

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:44.150 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to put myself
in the position of player i,

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:47.670 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to compare the
payoff I get from my strategy

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:49.830 align:middle line:84%
to the payoff I could
get if I deviated

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:51.230 align:middle line:90%
to a different strategy.

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:59.310 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to compare ui of
si star on the left to ui of si

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:01.310 align:middle line:90%
prime on the right.

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:04.470 align:middle line:84%
So I'm comparing--
I'm player i, and I'm

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:07.190 align:middle line:84%
saying if I choose
si star, what I'm

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:08.950 align:middle line:84%
supposed to do in
equilibrium, I want

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:12.150 align:middle line:84%
to do better weekly than if
I choose some other strategy

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:13.235 align:middle line:90%
si prime.

00:15:13.235 --> 00:15:14.610 align:middle line:84%
But I still have
to fill this in.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:18.150 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to say, given
the way that the other players

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:18.870 align:middle line:90%
are playing.

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:22.110 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to fill
in here is s negative i star

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.750 align:middle line:90%
and s negative i star.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.830 align:middle line:84%
I can't control how the
other people are playing.

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:28.010 align:middle line:90%
So I take that as fixed.

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:29.030 align:middle line:90%
I take that as given.

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:31.570 align:middle line:84%
Given The way that my
opponents are playing,

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:35.130 align:middle line:84%
I just do what I can control,
I compare the strategy

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:37.920 align:middle line:84%
I'm supposed to choose
to any deviation,

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:40.170 align:middle line:84%
and I require that the
strategy I'm supposed to choose

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:52.290 align:middle line:84%
is weakly better for
all si prime in si.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:56.530 align:middle line:84%
In particular, si prime
could just be si star,

00:15:56.530 --> 00:15:58.770 align:middle line:84%
but then this is going to
be satisfied automatically

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:00.603 align:middle line:84%
because both sides are
going to be the same.

00:16:00.603 --> 00:16:04.130 align:middle line:90%


00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:06.930 align:middle line:84%
So let's think of
another way to say this.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.490 align:middle line:84%
Another way to say this-- so
this is equivalent to this

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:10.810 align:middle line:90%
statement here--

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:26.250 align:middle line:84%
is that si star is a best
response to s negative i star.

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.210 align:middle line:84%
So another way of defining
a Nash equilibrium

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:30.250 align:middle line:84%
is to say that every
player I look at,

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:33.470 align:middle line:84%
if I look at the strategy that
player's using, that strategy

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:36.070 align:middle line:84%
better be a best response
to the strategies

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:37.648 align:middle line:84%
that the other
players are using.

00:16:37.648 --> 00:16:39.190 align:middle line:84%
Now, we have to be
a bit careful here

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:41.270 align:middle line:84%
because, when we first
defined a best response,

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:44.030 align:middle line:84%
we defined a best
response to a belief,

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.050 align:middle line:84%
not to a strategy
of the opponents.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:50.115 align:middle line:84%
But when I say a best response
to this strategy profile, what

00:16:50.115 --> 00:16:51.990 align:middle line:84%
I really mean, if I want
to be really formal,

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:54.670 align:middle line:84%
is I mean to the belief
that assigns probability

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:59.030 align:middle line:84%
1 to my opponents playing
this strategy profile, so to,

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:03.710 align:middle line:84%
more formally, the
belief beta negative I

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:10.310 align:middle line:84%
with beta negative i of s
negative i star equal 1.

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:14.650 align:middle line:84%
So if my belief is my opponents
are certain to play s negative

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:16.690 align:middle line:84%
i star, then the best
thing for me to do

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:20.510 align:middle line:84%
or a best thing for me
to do is to play si star.

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:22.950 align:middle line:84%
And here, you can immediately
see in the definition

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:25.250 align:middle line:84%
the consistency that
we're imposing on beliefs.

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:28.968 align:middle line:84%
So I think keeping in mind
this interpretation is crucial

00:17:28.968 --> 00:17:30.760 align:middle line:84%
because, when you first
see the definition,

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:32.302 align:middle line:84%
you might say, where
are the beliefs?

00:17:32.302 --> 00:17:35.400 align:middle line:84%
Well, because we're requiring
consistency of beliefs,

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:37.780 align:middle line:84%
the beliefs are the same as
what's actually happening.

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:40.660 align:middle line:84%
So s negative i star is how
the other players are actually

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:41.980 align:middle line:90%
playing.

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:44.343 align:middle line:84%
And beta negative i
is my correct belief

00:17:44.343 --> 00:17:46.760 align:middle line:84%
that the players are playing
exactly how they are playing.

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:49.780 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes we can lose track
of this distinction between play

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:51.820 align:middle line:84%
and beliefs because
our definition

00:17:51.820 --> 00:17:55.020 align:middle line:90%
requires that they agree.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:18:01.220 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we can give a bit of a
less mathematical frame on this.

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:03.220 align:middle line:84%
So another way we
might say it is we

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:15.740 align:middle line:84%
might say that no player
has a strictly profitable

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:17.200 align:middle line:90%
unilateral deviation.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:24.220 align:middle line:90%


00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:26.800 align:middle line:84%
So let's break down
what this means.

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:29.680 align:middle line:84%
No player-- so let's
focus on a single player--

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:33.080 align:middle line:84%
should have a strictly
profitable unilateral deviation.

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:35.800 align:middle line:84%
So a deviation for a
player is a strategy

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:39.640 align:middle line:84%
that deviates from or is
different from their conjectured

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:40.700 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium strategy.

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:43.040 align:middle line:84%
So we start with si
star for player i,

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.880 align:middle line:84%
and we contemplate an
alternative strategy

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:47.800 align:middle line:90%
for player i.

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:50.600 align:middle line:84%
A unilateral deviation
means I'm the only one who's

00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:52.160 align:middle line:90%
changing my strategy.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:53.960 align:middle line:84%
And this is I think
the key, maybe

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:55.960 align:middle line:84%
the most important idea
that Nash introduced

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:58.760 align:middle line:84%
was that, when I'm reasoning
about other people,

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:00.840 align:middle line:84%
I should take their behavior
and their strategies

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:03.760 align:middle line:84%
as fixed because I can't
directly control what they do.

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:05.960 align:middle line:84%
So when I contemplate
deviating, I only

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:08.040 align:middle line:84%
consider a change
in my strategy.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:10.760 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that up
here, in this inequality,

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:12.640 align:middle line:84%
that the way that my
opponents are playing

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:15.080 align:middle line:84%
is the same on both
sides of the inequality.

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:16.800 align:middle line:84%
The only difference
between the two sides

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:19.560 align:middle line:90%
is how I'm playing as player i.

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:23.180 align:middle line:84%
And this strategy cannot
be-- this deviation cannot be

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:24.320 align:middle line:90%
strictly profitable.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:26.487 align:middle line:84%
It could be that you have
a deviation that gives you

00:19:26.487 --> 00:19:29.160 align:middle line:84%
the same payoff as your
equilibrium strategy,

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:31.700 align:middle line:84%
but it can't be that you can
deviate and do strictly better.

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:34.060 align:middle line:84%
And again, we're
using profitability

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:35.980 align:middle line:84%
not in the sense of
a monetary payoff

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:37.440 align:middle line:90%
but in the sense of utilities.

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.580 align:middle line:84%
So a profitable
deviation is a deviation

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:42.620 align:middle line:84%
that gives me a higher utility,
a strictly higher expected

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:43.320 align:middle line:90%
utility.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:46.020 align:middle line:90%


00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:49.940 align:middle line:84%
So let's go back to
this game and see

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:53.640 align:middle line:84%
if we can find the Nash
equilibria of this game.

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:57.940 align:middle line:84%
Any guesses on what the Nash
equilibria of BOS this game

00:19:57.940 --> 00:19:59.658 align:middle line:84%
are going to be based
on the definition

00:19:59.658 --> 00:20:01.200 align:middle line:84%
or based on the
things we've written?

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:01.700 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:02.962 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Both [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:02.962 --> 00:20:04.420 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right,
so these are going

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:06.860 align:middle line:90%
to be the two Nash equilibria.

00:20:06.860 --> 00:20:07.760 align:middle line:90%
How can we see this?

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:11.140 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's suppose we're both
going to the Celtics game.

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:14.380 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone have a profitable
unilateral deviation?

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:16.540 align:middle line:84%
No, because if I were
to unilaterally deviate,

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:19.020 align:middle line:84%
we're together at the
Celtics game, if I deviate,

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:20.840 align:middle line:84%
I'm by myself with
the other game,

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:22.680 align:middle line:90%
so I'm certainly worse off.

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:25.140 align:middle line:84%
And the same is true if
we go to the Red Sox game.

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:26.960 align:middle line:84%
If we're both going
to the Red Sox game,

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:30.440 align:middle line:84%
if I unilaterally deviate,
I'm going to be worse off.

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:33.880 align:middle line:84%
Now, of course, if we're both
going to the Red Sox game

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:37.000 align:middle line:84%
and I'm player 1, I would
much rather that we both

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:40.160 align:middle line:84%
go to the Celtics game
because I'm a Celtics fan.

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.840 align:middle line:84%
But moving from here to here
is not a unilateral deviation.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:46.280 align:middle line:84%
All I can control is
whether I go to the Red Sox

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:47.340 align:middle line:90%
game or the Celtics game.

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:50.007 align:middle line:84%
Of course, I would love to force
you to come to the Celtics game

00:20:50.007 --> 00:20:52.720 align:middle line:84%
with me, but that's not
something I can do in this game.

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:54.800 align:middle line:84%
So that's the
intuitive explanation.

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:58.080 align:middle line:84%
Mathematically, we can see that
both numbers are underlined.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:01.200 align:middle line:84%
And that's actually a great
way to compute Nash equilibria

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:02.480 align:middle line:90%
in games like this.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:05.160 align:middle line:90%
So we go through for player 1.

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:07.960 align:middle line:84%
We underline all of
their best responses.

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:10.120 align:middle line:84%
Then we go through
player 2 and underline

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:11.840 align:middle line:90%
all of their best responses.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:15.620 align:middle line:84%
And we look-- so I guess this is
an algorithm for finding them.

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:18.160 align:middle line:90%


00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:23.240 align:middle line:84%
So step one is underline
best responses.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:25.802 align:middle line:90%


00:21:25.802 --> 00:21:27.260 align:middle line:84%
And it's crucial
that you underline

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:31.420 align:middle line:84%
both players best responses and
underline all best responses.

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:36.640 align:middle line:84%
So let me say all best
responses for both players.

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:43.580 align:middle line:90%


00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:48.980 align:middle line:84%
And then step 2 is you
just check which cells--

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:53.820 align:middle line:84%
or which boxes in the matrix
have both numbers underlined.

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:57.940 align:middle line:90%
So look for cells--

00:21:57.940 --> 00:21:58.720 align:middle line:90%
what is a cell?

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:01.760 align:middle line:84%
Well, a cell in this context
is just a strategy profile.

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:02.760 align:middle line:90%
So let's remember that.

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:17.400 align:middle line:90%


00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:19.700 align:middle line:84%
And if you think
through this carefully,

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:22.460 align:middle line:84%
you'll see this is just another
way of saying the definition.

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:24.380 align:middle line:84%
If both cells are
underlined-- well,

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:26.340 align:middle line:84%
if the first player's
number is underlined,

00:22:26.340 --> 00:22:28.400 align:middle line:84%
that precisely means
player 1 is playing

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:31.160 align:middle line:84%
a best response to
player 2's strategy.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:33.140 align:middle line:84%
And if player two's
number is underlined,

00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:35.640 align:middle line:84%
that means player 2 is playing
a best response to player 1's

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:36.320 align:middle line:90%
strategy.

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:38.640 align:middle line:84%
And that's exactly our
definition of a Nash

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:41.160 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium, at least
in a two-player game.

00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:44.160 align:middle line:84%
So this is a good way going
through and finding and checking

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:47.343 align:middle line:90%
for Nash equilibrium.

00:22:47.343 --> 00:22:48.760 align:middle line:84%
So now I think
it's helpful to try

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:55.040 align:middle line:84%
to understand the relationship
between some of the solution

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:59.040 align:middle line:84%
concepts and some of the
definitions we've given so far.

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:04.320 align:middle line:84%
So so far, we have three, I
think, important definitions.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:06.340 align:middle line:90%
One was rationalizability.

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:12.120 align:middle line:90%


00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:14.720 align:middle line:84%
And we represented the set
of rationalizable strategy

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:15.300 align:middle line:90%
profiles.

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:17.700 align:middle line:90%
We called that s infinity.

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:20.040 align:middle line:84%
Then another important
notion is Nash Equilibrium.

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:22.740 align:middle line:90%


00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:25.060 align:middle line:84%
And let's call
this NE, so let me

00:23:25.060 --> 00:23:29.100 align:middle line:84%
think of NE as the set of Nash
Equilibrium strategy profiles

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:32.260 align:middle line:90%
in a game.

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:37.060 align:middle line:84%
So just to be clear,
this is a subset of S.

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:38.920 align:middle line:90%
What kind of objects are these?

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:40.600 align:middle line:90%
These are strategy profiles.

00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:42.740 align:middle line:84%
So rationalizable
strategy profiles

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:45.820 align:middle line:84%
live in the set of
all strategy profiles.

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:49.340 align:middle line:84%
Then the set of Nash equilibria
that's also a subset of big S

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:52.620 align:middle line:84%
because a Nash equilibrium
is also a strategy profile.

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:54.660 align:middle line:84%
And then we had another
notion, remember,

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:56.720 align:middle line:84%
which was Dominant-Strategy
Equilibrium.

00:23:56.720 --> 00:24:01.100 align:middle line:90%


00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:03.140 align:middle line:90%
And we'll call that DSE.

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:08.380 align:middle line:84%
And that's also a subset
of S. So we have three sets

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:09.980 align:middle line:90%
of strategy profiles here.

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:12.740 align:middle line:84%
Let's remember a
dominant-strategy equilibrium,

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:14.580 align:middle line:84%
if you recall from
the dominance lecture,

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:18.440 align:middle line:84%
was a strategy profile where
each player's strategy is

00:24:18.440 --> 00:24:21.480 align:middle line:90%
a weakly dominant strategy.

00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:23.662 align:middle line:90%
That was the definition.

00:24:23.662 --> 00:24:25.120 align:middle line:84%
So now what we'd
like to understand

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:26.740 align:middle line:84%
is the relationship
between them.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:31.560 align:middle line:90%


00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:33.760 align:middle line:84%
If something's a dominant
strategy equilibrium,

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:34.938 align:middle line:90%
is it a Nash equilibrium?

00:24:34.938 --> 00:24:36.980 align:middle line:84%
If it's rationalizable,
is it a Nash equilibrium?

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:37.840 align:middle line:90%
And so on.

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:39.345 align:middle line:84%
I think a good way
to get started,

00:24:39.345 --> 00:24:41.220 align:middle line:84%
when you're approaching
a question like this,

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:42.760 align:middle line:84%
is to work through an
example and see if you can

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:44.280 align:middle line:90%
get some inspiration from that.

00:24:44.280 --> 00:24:46.320 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at
our BOS example.

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:50.720 align:middle line:84%
So here, the set of
rationalizable strategies

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:53.380 align:middle line:90%
was all four strategies.

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:58.520 align:middle line:90%


00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:00.560 align:middle line:84%
The set of Nash
equilibria in this game

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:04.540 align:middle line:84%
over here with just two strategy
profiles right, CC and RR.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:11.280 align:middle line:90%


00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:14.000 align:middle line:84%
What about the set of
dominant-strategy equilibria?

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:16.980 align:middle line:84%
Which profiles were
dominant-strategy equilibria

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:17.800 align:middle line:90%
in this game?

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:23.500 align:middle line:90%


00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:25.420 align:middle line:90%
Any thoughts?

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:28.420 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's go
through for player 1.

00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:29.080 align:middle line:90%
Let's check.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:31.400 align:middle line:84%
Is C a dominant
strategy for player 1?

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:33.340 align:middle line:84%
That is, does C always
do better than R

00:25:33.340 --> 00:25:36.140 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes do
strictly better?

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:38.740 align:middle line:84%
Well, no, because if the
other player plays R,

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:43.020 align:middle line:84%
then C does worse than R. C
gives me 0, and R gives me 1.

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:45.620 align:middle line:90%
So C does not weakly dominate R.

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:47.240 align:middle line:84%
And now let's ask
the other question.

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:50.020 align:middle line:90%
Does R weakly dominate C?

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:52.805 align:middle line:84%
Well, no, because if my opponent
goes to the Celtics game,

00:25:52.805 --> 00:25:54.180 align:middle line:84%
I'd rather go to
the Celtics game

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:55.780 align:middle line:90%
than go to the Red Sox game.

00:25:55.780 --> 00:26:01.082 align:middle line:84%
So neither does C dominate
R, nor does R dominate C.

00:26:01.082 --> 00:26:02.540 align:middle line:84%
And that means that
neither of them

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:05.320 align:middle line:84%
is weakly dominant because
if it was weakly dominant,

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:07.460 align:middle line:84%
it would have to weakly
dominate everything else.

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:10.200 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, we have no
dominant strategy equilibria

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:11.400 align:middle line:90%
in this game.

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:14.837 align:middle line:90%
So here, we have none.

00:26:14.837 --> 00:26:16.420 align:middle line:84%
Or we might say we
have the empty set.

00:26:16.420 --> 00:26:20.600 align:middle line:90%


00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:22.160 align:middle line:84%
So at least in this
example, we see

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:24.320 align:middle line:84%
that Nash equilibrium
is more demanding

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:25.760 align:middle line:90%
than rationalizability.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:28.920 align:middle line:84%
The Nash equilibria are a subset
of the rationalizable strategy

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:31.800 align:middle line:84%
profiles, and
dominant-strategy equilibrium

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:34.395 align:middle line:90%
is even more demanding still.

00:26:34.395 --> 00:26:36.020 align:middle line:84%
It's a bit weird to
say it in this case

00:26:36.020 --> 00:26:38.640 align:middle line:84%
because there are none of
them, but it's vacuously true

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:41.200 align:middle line:84%
that every dominant strategy
equilibrium is a Nash

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:41.780 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:26:41.780 --> 00:26:42.988 align:middle line:90%
They just aren't any of them.

00:26:42.988 --> 00:26:45.860 align:middle line:84%
But if there were one, it
would be a Nash equilibrium.

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:47.320 align:middle line:84%
So that gives us
maybe a conjecture

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:50.800 align:middle line:84%
based on this example about
the relationship between them.

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:53.900 align:middle line:84%
And I chose the example, so
we wouldn't be led astray.

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:57.680 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, this is a
result that, in general, we

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:03.820 align:middle line:84%
have DSE subset of NE
subset of S infinity,

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:06.760 align:middle line:84%
and maybe I'll write subset of
S just to remind us the space

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:09.480 align:middle line:90%
that we're in over here.

00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:12.780 align:middle line:84%
So let's understand what these
subset relationships mean.

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:14.080 align:middle line:90%
DSE is a set.

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:16.700 align:middle line:84%
It's the set of all
dominant-strategy equilibria

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:18.900 align:middle line:84%
which is a set of
strategy profiles.

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:21.220 align:middle line:84%
This is another set,
and this is another set.

00:27:21.220 --> 00:27:25.420 align:middle line:84%
So this inclusion tells me every
dominant-strategy equilibrium

00:27:25.420 --> 00:27:28.340 align:middle line:84%
is necessarily a
Nash equilibrium.

00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:30.340 align:middle line:84%
The converse is not
necessarily true.

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:32.860 align:middle line:84%
It could be that something's
a Nash equilibrium but not

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:34.620 align:middle line:90%
a dominant-strategy equilibrium.

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:37.220 align:middle line:84%
Can you come up with an
example of a Nash equilibrium

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:39.920 align:middle line:84%
that's not a dominant-strategy
equilibrium, maybe in this game?

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:43.460 align:middle line:90%


00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:43.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:27:43.960 --> 00:27:46.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
is a Nash equilibrium,

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:48.585 align:middle line:84%
but it's not a
dominant strategy.

00:27:48.585 --> 00:27:49.960 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right,
and same with R,

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:51.220 align:middle line:84%
so here, it's easy
because there are

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:52.680 align:middle line:90%
no dominant-strategy equilibria.

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:54.540 align:middle line:84%
So both of the
Nash equilibria are

00:27:54.540 --> 00:27:56.900 align:middle line:84%
examples of Nash
equilibria that are not

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:59.460 align:middle line:90%
dominant-strategy equilibria.

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:01.900 align:middle line:84%
And then, similarly,
we have, there

00:28:01.900 --> 00:28:04.260 align:middle line:84%
are some rationalizable
strategy profiles

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:05.620 align:middle line:90%
that are not Nash equilibrium.

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:10.480 align:middle line:84%
In this example, CR
and RC are two profiles

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:15.000 align:middle line:84%
that are rationalizable
but not Nash equilibrium.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:17.300 align:middle line:84%
Now, sometimes these
sets could agree,

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:19.300 align:middle line:84%
but sometimes this set
could be strictly bigger.

00:28:19.300 --> 00:28:22.480 align:middle line:84%
But our claim here is just that
everything in here is in here,

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:23.928 align:middle line:84%
and everything in
here is in here.

00:28:23.928 --> 00:28:26.220 align:middle line:84%
And now we'd like to go
through the reasoning for that,

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:28.320 align:middle line:84%
but are there any questions on
what I mean by this statement

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:29.780 align:middle line:84%
before we go through
the argument?

00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:35.720 align:middle line:90%


00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:37.580 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let's try to
think through it.

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:41.345 align:middle line:90%
So here's the argument.

00:28:41.345 --> 00:28:43.220 align:middle line:84%
There's two different
things we want to show.

00:28:43.220 --> 00:28:45.820 align:middle line:84%
I mean, let's be clear,
this is just for context.

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:47.855 align:middle line:90%
This relationship is obvious.

00:28:47.855 --> 00:28:50.480 align:middle line:84%
It's just, by definition, every
rationalizable strategy profile

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:51.700 align:middle line:90%
is a strategy profile.

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:53.240 align:middle line:90%
So we have two things to check.

00:28:53.240 --> 00:28:54.340 align:middle line:90%
So let's do the first one.

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:59.800 align:middle line:84%
The first one is that
DSE is a subset of NE.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:01.303 align:middle line:84%
So that's a mathy
way of saying it,

00:29:01.303 --> 00:29:02.720 align:middle line:84%
but what we mean
is if you give me

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:05.510 align:middle line:84%
a dominant-strategy
equilibrium of some game,

00:29:05.510 --> 00:29:08.790 align:middle line:84%
I know that it must be a Nash
equilibrium of that game.

00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:10.730 align:middle line:84%
So let's see how
we can show that.

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:14.150 align:middle line:84%
So first, let's start with a
dominant-strategy equilibrium.

00:29:14.150 --> 00:29:23.150 align:middle line:84%
So suppose S star, which I'll
write S1 star up to SN star,

00:29:23.150 --> 00:29:24.610 align:middle line:90%
is a DSE.

00:29:24.610 --> 00:29:25.610 align:middle line:90%
That's a starting point.

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:27.710 align:middle line:84%
We're just going to
take a strategy profile

00:29:27.710 --> 00:29:29.470 align:middle line:84%
and make the
assumption that it is

00:29:29.470 --> 00:29:32.190 align:middle line:90%
a dominant-strategy equilibrium.

00:29:32.190 --> 00:29:33.990 align:middle line:84%
And we want to
now reason through

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:39.590 align:middle line:84%
why this strategy profile must
also be a Nash equilibrium.

00:29:39.590 --> 00:29:42.670 align:middle line:84%
Well, to show that it's
a Nash equilibrium,

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:46.230 align:middle line:84%
we have to check
that no player has

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:48.950 align:middle line:84%
a profitable
unilateral deviation.

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:51.950 align:middle line:90%
So let's consider a player.

00:29:51.950 --> 00:29:59.670 align:middle line:84%
So that means that
for, each player i,

00:29:59.670 --> 00:30:02.090 align:middle line:84%
let's consider player
i, and let's also

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:04.850 align:middle line:84%
consider a potential
deviation by player i.

00:30:04.850 --> 00:30:17.250 align:middle line:84%
So for each player i and
each strategy si prime,

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:20.810 align:middle line:90%
what do we want to show?

00:30:20.810 --> 00:30:23.850 align:middle line:84%
We want to show that
this deviation is not

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:26.530 align:middle line:90%
profitable for player i.

00:30:26.530 --> 00:30:29.090 align:middle line:84%
That is, we want to
show that, for player i,

00:30:29.090 --> 00:30:34.272 align:middle line:84%
it's better for them to play
si star than to play si prime,

00:30:34.272 --> 00:30:36.230 align:middle line:84%
given the way that their
opponents are playing.

00:30:36.230 --> 00:30:39.140 align:middle line:90%


00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:41.170 align:middle line:90%
But that's what we want to show.

00:30:41.170 --> 00:30:44.150 align:middle line:84%
All we know is that this is a
dominant-strategy equilibrium.

00:30:44.150 --> 00:30:46.788 align:middle line:90%
So what's something useful--

00:30:46.788 --> 00:30:48.330 align:middle line:84%
do you have any
ideas of how we could

00:30:48.330 --> 00:30:50.930 align:middle line:84%
use the fact that this is a
dominant-strategy equilibrium

00:30:50.930 --> 00:30:51.830 align:middle line:90%
to get us started?

00:30:51.830 --> 00:30:54.890 align:middle line:84%
What do we know
about si star, given

00:30:54.890 --> 00:30:57.350 align:middle line:84%
that s star is a
dominant-strategy equilibrium?

00:30:57.350 --> 00:31:02.510 align:middle line:90%


00:31:02.510 --> 00:31:03.430 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:03.430 --> 00:31:06.510 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: si star [INAUDIBLE]?

00:31:06.510 --> 00:31:08.870 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yeah, so let's
go through it in turn.

00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:11.090 align:middle line:84%
Because it's a
dominant-strategy equilibrium,

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:15.070 align:middle line:84%
we know that si star
is weakly dominant.

00:31:15.070 --> 00:31:17.110 align:middle line:84%
And what does it mean
to be weakly dominant?

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:20.590 align:middle line:84%
It means that si star weakly
dominates every other strategy

00:31:20.590 --> 00:31:22.270 align:middle line:90%
of player i.

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:25.590 align:middle line:84%
But in particular, that
means si star weakly

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:27.270 align:middle line:84%
dominates si prime
because that's

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:29.190 align:middle line:90%
another strategy of player i.

00:31:29.190 --> 00:31:38.492 align:middle line:84%
So we know that si star
weakly dominates si prime.

00:31:38.492 --> 00:31:39.450 align:middle line:90%
We know more than that.

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:41.450 align:middle line:84%
But here, we're just
throwing away information.

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:43.590 align:middle line:84%
If si star weakly
dominates everything,

00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:48.270 align:middle line:84%
in particular, it weakly
dominates si prime.

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:50.330 align:middle line:84%
And now can we reach
the conclusion we want?

00:31:50.330 --> 00:31:53.390 align:middle line:90%


00:31:53.390 --> 00:31:56.870 align:middle line:84%
Well, yeah, if si star
weakly dominates si prime,

00:31:56.870 --> 00:32:01.310 align:middle line:84%
that means si star always does
weakly better than si prime,

00:32:01.310 --> 00:32:03.730 align:middle line:84%
no matter how my
opponents are playing,

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:06.170 align:middle line:84%
so if that's true and
sometimes strictly better.

00:32:06.170 --> 00:32:08.630 align:middle line:84%
So if that's true no matter
how my opponents are playing,

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:10.690 align:middle line:84%
it's certainly true
in the particular way

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:12.450 align:middle line:90%
my opponents are playing now.

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:20.130 align:middle line:84%
So that tells me that ui
of si star si negative--

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:23.410 align:middle line:84%
s negative i star is
greater than or equal to ui

00:32:23.410 --> 00:32:28.090 align:middle line:90%
si prime s negative i star.

00:32:28.090 --> 00:32:31.050 align:middle line:84%
So because si star weekly
dominance si prime,

00:32:31.050 --> 00:32:33.770 align:middle line:84%
this inequality would be true
whatever I put on both sides

00:32:33.770 --> 00:32:34.270 align:middle line:90%
here.

00:32:34.270 --> 00:32:36.890 align:middle line:84%
I could put any as long as I put
the same thing on both sides.

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:39.650 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, if I put the
way my opponents are actually

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:42.090 align:middle line:84%
playing, this is
going to be true.

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:47.770 align:middle line:84%
And this precisely means
that si star is a best

00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:50.770 align:middle line:90%
response to s negative i star.

00:32:50.770 --> 00:33:04.190 align:middle line:84%
So I conclude si star is a best
response to s negative i star?

00:33:04.190 --> 00:33:06.157 align:middle line:84%
Well, I have reason
for player i,

00:33:06.157 --> 00:33:07.990 align:middle line:84%
but I can go through
the exact same argument

00:33:07.990 --> 00:33:09.390 align:middle line:90%
for all the other players.

00:33:09.390 --> 00:33:11.870 align:middle line:84%
So not only is player i
playing a best response

00:33:11.870 --> 00:33:13.470 align:middle line:84%
to the way everyone
else is playing,

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:15.670 align:middle line:84%
but every single player
i is playing a best

00:33:15.670 --> 00:33:17.950 align:middle line:84%
response to the way
everyone else is playing.

00:33:17.950 --> 00:33:24.368 align:middle line:84%
So I'll say, therefore, s
star is a Nash equilibrium.

00:33:24.368 --> 00:33:26.410 align:middle line:84%
So if you're not so familiar
with writing proofs,

00:33:26.410 --> 00:33:28.530 align:middle line:84%
this kind of reasoning
may be a little tricky.

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:29.322 align:middle line:90%
Let me pause there.

00:33:29.322 --> 00:33:32.377 align:middle line:90%
Are any questions on this?

00:33:32.377 --> 00:33:34.710 align:middle line:84%
So it's really just about
being organized and going step

00:33:34.710 --> 00:33:35.630 align:middle line:90%
by step.

00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:37.170 align:middle line:84%
None of the steps
is too difficult,

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:38.950 align:middle line:84%
but you have to keep
in mind where you're

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:41.830 align:middle line:84%
going, where you're
starting, and carefully

00:33:41.830 --> 00:33:42.970 align:middle line:90%
go through the logic of it.

00:33:42.970 --> 00:33:45.870 align:middle line:90%


00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:47.790 align:middle line:84%
But the basic idea
is pretty simple.

00:33:47.790 --> 00:33:51.230 align:middle line:84%
If what I'm doing is best no
matter how my opponents play,

00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:53.190 align:middle line:84%
it's certainly best,
given the particular way

00:33:53.190 --> 00:33:54.440 align:middle line:90%
that my opponents are playing.

00:33:54.440 --> 00:33:57.970 align:middle line:84%
And that's the
high-level idea of this.

00:33:57.970 --> 00:34:01.690 align:middle line:84%
OK, so now let's try to
show the second part.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:04.890 align:middle line:84%
So I have shown that every
dominant strategy equilibrium

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:06.185 align:middle line:90%
is a Nash equilibrium.

00:34:06.185 --> 00:34:07.810 align:middle line:84%
Notice, I didn't show
that there exists

00:34:07.810 --> 00:34:09.350 align:middle line:90%
a dominant-strategy equilibrium.

00:34:09.350 --> 00:34:11.188 align:middle line:84%
My starting point
here was let's take

00:34:11.188 --> 00:34:13.230 align:middle line:84%
something that's a
dominant-strategy equilibrium.

00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:15.230 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's no dominant
strategy equilibrium.

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:15.949 align:middle line:90%
That's OK.

00:34:15.949 --> 00:34:18.210 align:middle line:84%
But I'm saying if there
is one, it must also

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:20.570 align:middle line:90%
be a Nash equilibrium.

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:24.770 align:middle line:84%
And now let's go see if
we can do this next step.

00:34:24.770 --> 00:34:28.330 align:middle line:84%
So part 2 is to
show that every Nash

00:34:28.330 --> 00:34:32.770 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium is rationalizable.

00:34:32.770 --> 00:34:35.090 align:middle line:84%
So if I start with something
that's Nash equilibrium,

00:34:35.090 --> 00:34:38.650 align:middle line:84%
it better be a rationalizable
strategy profile.

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:41.770 align:middle line:84%
Now this seems a bit
hard because the way

00:34:41.770 --> 00:34:43.712 align:middle line:84%
we defined S infinity
was really tricky.

00:34:43.712 --> 00:34:45.670 align:middle line:84%
We went through this
whole iterative procedure.

00:34:45.670 --> 00:34:46.503 align:middle line:90%
We had an algorithm.

00:34:46.503 --> 00:34:48.449 align:middle line:84%
We had to go through
infinitely many steps.

00:34:48.449 --> 00:34:53.670 align:middle line:84%
So do you have any ideas
about how we might show this?

00:34:53.670 --> 00:34:55.429 align:middle line:84%
Any mathematical
technique that might be

00:34:55.429 --> 00:34:58.090 align:middle line:90%
useful for this kind of proof?

00:34:58.090 --> 00:34:58.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:00.657 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Proof
by contradiction.

00:35:00.657 --> 00:35:01.490 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: We could--

00:35:01.490 --> 00:35:03.070 align:middle line:84%
I think a proof by
contradiction would work, yeah,

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:03.970 align:middle line:90%
but I saw another hand.

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:04.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:04.730 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Induction.

00:35:04.730 --> 00:35:05.690 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Induction
is a good idea.

00:35:05.690 --> 00:35:07.150 align:middle line:90%
Contradiction would work.

00:35:07.150 --> 00:35:08.210 align:middle line:84%
A lot of things, you
could convert it.

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:10.127 align:middle line:84%
But I think induction
is a really natural idea

00:35:10.127 --> 00:35:13.430 align:middle line:84%
because we've defined
S infinity inductively.

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:15.130 align:middle line:90%
So we'd like to use induction.

00:35:15.130 --> 00:35:17.172 align:middle line:84%
And if you haven't heard
of induction, that's OK,

00:35:17.172 --> 00:35:18.172 align:middle line:90%
I'll go through it.

00:35:18.172 --> 00:35:19.630 align:middle line:84%
OK, but let's first
just understand

00:35:19.630 --> 00:35:21.225 align:middle line:90%
what we're trying to do.

00:35:21.225 --> 00:35:23.350 align:middle line:84%
We're going to follow the
same procedure as before.

00:35:23.350 --> 00:35:27.070 align:middle line:90%
So suppose s star--

00:35:27.070 --> 00:35:28.830 align:middle line:90%
this is a strategy profile--

00:35:28.830 --> 00:35:31.170 align:middle line:90%
is a Nash equilibrium.

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:35.590 align:middle line:90%


00:35:35.590 --> 00:35:36.910 align:middle line:90%
What do we want to do?

00:35:36.910 --> 00:35:41.870 align:middle line:84%
We want to show that s
star is rationalizable.

00:35:41.870 --> 00:35:50.090 align:middle line:84%
So we want to show that
s star is in S infinity.

00:35:50.090 --> 00:35:52.770 align:middle line:90%


00:35:52.770 --> 00:35:55.370 align:middle line:84%
But doing that directly
is hard because S infinity

00:35:55.370 --> 00:35:58.410 align:middle line:84%
is only the result of
this iterative procedure.

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:01.690 align:middle line:84%
But remember, from
over here, the way

00:36:01.690 --> 00:36:04.690 align:middle line:84%
we got S infinity is we just
kept making the set smaller

00:36:04.690 --> 00:36:06.290 align:middle line:90%
and smaller and smaller.

00:36:06.290 --> 00:36:09.370 align:middle line:84%
So one way-- or what's
equivalent to showing that s

00:36:09.370 --> 00:36:11.290 align:middle line:90%
star is in S infinity--

00:36:11.290 --> 00:36:17.655 align:middle line:84%
so I'll say, or equivalently
and easier to show

00:36:17.655 --> 00:36:24.050 align:middle line:90%
S star is in Sk for all k.

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:25.470 align:middle line:90%
So let's just say this in words.

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:27.450 align:middle line:84%
We want to show
that s star survives

00:36:27.450 --> 00:36:30.028 align:middle line:84%
every round of elimination
and makes it till the end.

00:36:30.028 --> 00:36:31.570 align:middle line:84%
Well, the way we
show that is we just

00:36:31.570 --> 00:36:34.253 align:middle line:84%
show it makes it to the first
step and the second step

00:36:34.253 --> 00:36:36.670 align:middle line:84%
and the third step and the
fourth step and the fifth step,

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:38.230 align:middle line:90%
and it never gets thrown out.

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:40.570 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, it must survive
all the way to the end.

00:36:40.570 --> 00:36:43.970 align:middle line:84%
So we want to show that this
strategy profile must survive

00:36:43.970 --> 00:36:47.530 align:middle line:90%
every round of elimination.

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:49.870 align:middle line:84%
And since we're showing
something for every k,

00:36:49.870 --> 00:36:51.210 align:middle line:84%
this is where we're
going to use induction.

00:36:51.210 --> 00:36:52.335 align:middle line:90%
So what do we want to show?

00:36:52.335 --> 00:36:58.190 align:middle line:84%
We want to show that, first--
so there's a base case of k

00:36:58.190 --> 00:36:59.070 align:middle line:90%
equals 0.

00:36:59.070 --> 00:37:01.750 align:middle line:84%
We want to show first
that s star is in there

00:37:01.750 --> 00:37:03.293 align:middle line:90%
after the zeroth round.

00:37:03.293 --> 00:37:04.710 align:middle line:84%
And then we have
an inductive step

00:37:04.710 --> 00:37:07.190 align:middle line:84%
that says if it's made
it to the k-th round,

00:37:07.190 --> 00:37:10.262 align:middle line:84%
It's also going to make it
to the k plus first round.

00:37:10.262 --> 00:37:11.970 align:middle line:84%
And then we can just
tie it all together.

00:37:11.970 --> 00:37:14.310 align:middle line:84%
If we know it makes it
to the zeroth round,

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:15.850 align:middle line:84%
then it must make
it to the first.

00:37:15.850 --> 00:37:18.470 align:middle line:84%
And if it makes it the first,
it must make it to the second

00:37:18.470 --> 00:37:18.970 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:21.310 align:middle line:84%
And we can just chain
our reasoning together.

00:37:21.310 --> 00:37:25.870 align:middle line:84%
So for the base case of k
equals 0, what we need to show

00:37:25.870 --> 00:37:30.973 align:middle line:90%
is that s star is in S0.

00:37:30.973 --> 00:37:32.390 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I'll put
a question mark

00:37:32.390 --> 00:37:33.770 align:middle line:90%
because this is not a claim.

00:37:33.770 --> 00:37:35.610 align:middle line:90%
I'm not saying it's true.

00:37:35.610 --> 00:37:36.990 align:middle line:90%
It's what we're trying to show.

00:37:36.990 --> 00:37:40.220 align:middle line:90%
So is s star in S0?

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:42.470 align:middle line:84%
Well, yeah, because S0 is
just the set of all strategy

00:37:42.470 --> 00:37:44.110 align:middle line:90%
profiles.

00:37:44.110 --> 00:37:49.170 align:middle line:84%
And s star is a strategy
profile, so that's pretty easy.

00:37:49.170 --> 00:37:51.250 align:middle line:90%
This is just by definition.

00:37:51.250 --> 00:37:54.210 align:middle line:84%
The heart of the proof
is the inductive step.

00:37:54.210 --> 00:37:55.950 align:middle line:90%
So here's the inductive step.

00:37:55.950 --> 00:38:00.730 align:middle line:90%


00:38:00.730 --> 00:38:02.250 align:middle line:84%
What we want to
show is, in general,

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:05.370 align:middle line:84%
is if s star makes it
through the first k rounds,

00:38:05.370 --> 00:38:07.230 align:middle line:84%
then it must make
it one more round.

00:38:07.230 --> 00:38:09.610 align:middle line:84%
It must also make it to
the k plus first round.

00:38:09.610 --> 00:38:11.450 align:middle line:84%
So what we Want To
Show-- and maybe I'll

00:38:11.450 --> 00:38:13.210 align:middle line:84%
say WTS because it's
always important

00:38:13.210 --> 00:38:15.210 align:middle line:84%
it proves to be clear
about what you're assuming

00:38:15.210 --> 00:38:16.502 align:middle line:90%
and what you're trying to show.

00:38:16.502 --> 00:38:19.730 align:middle line:84%
So WTS is just an
abbreviation for Want To Show.

00:38:19.730 --> 00:38:26.890 align:middle line:84%
What I Want To Show is
that if s star is in Sk,

00:38:26.890 --> 00:38:34.290 align:middle line:90%
then s star is in Sk plus 1.

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:37.010 align:middle line:84%
So if I haven't thrown
it out by the k-th round,

00:38:37.010 --> 00:38:39.350 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to throw it out
at the k plus first round.

00:38:39.350 --> 00:38:41.923 align:middle line:90%


00:38:41.923 --> 00:38:43.590 align:middle line:84%
I think if we just
look at it like this,

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:45.610 align:middle line:84%
it can be a little
tricky because, remember,

00:38:45.610 --> 00:38:48.110 align:middle line:84%
the way we defined
this procedure was we

00:38:48.110 --> 00:38:49.850 align:middle line:90%
had to reason player by player.

00:38:49.850 --> 00:38:51.670 align:middle line:84%
So let's write that
more explicitly.

00:38:51.670 --> 00:38:54.950 align:middle line:84%
What does it mean for this
strategy profile to be in Sk?

00:38:54.950 --> 00:38:57.870 align:middle line:84%
It means the i-th
component of that profile,

00:38:57.870 --> 00:39:01.570 align:middle line:84%
player i's strategy is in si
k for each of the players i.

00:39:01.570 --> 00:39:03.690 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's
a bit more concrete.

00:39:03.690 --> 00:39:07.110 align:middle line:90%
So that means that si star--

00:39:07.110 --> 00:39:10.510 align:middle line:84%
that is, remember, s star
is a profile of strategies.

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:13.670 align:middle line:84%
si star is the strategy that
player i is actually using.

00:39:13.670 --> 00:39:18.070 align:middle line:90%
That must be in si k.

00:39:18.070 --> 00:39:19.610 align:middle line:90%
And this is true for all i.

00:39:19.610 --> 00:39:23.990 align:middle line:90%


00:39:23.990 --> 00:39:28.150 align:middle line:84%
And now, symmetrically,
what we're trying to show

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:33.970 align:middle line:84%
is that si star is in
si k plus 1 for all i.

00:39:33.970 --> 00:39:37.670 align:middle line:90%


00:39:37.670 --> 00:39:41.445 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we go through
proofs and arguments,

00:39:41.445 --> 00:39:43.320 align:middle line:84%
we only go through the
right way of doing it,

00:39:43.320 --> 00:39:44.737 align:middle line:84%
and I think you
can sometimes lose

00:39:44.737 --> 00:39:47.580 align:middle line:84%
sight of how hard
it is because you

00:39:47.580 --> 00:39:49.660 align:middle line:90%
don't show all the wrong paths.

00:39:49.660 --> 00:39:51.560 align:middle line:84%
And there's an easy
mistake people make here.

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:53.100 align:middle line:84%
What's really
tempting at this point

00:39:53.100 --> 00:39:55.740 align:middle line:84%
is to say, OK, let me
reason player by player.

00:39:55.740 --> 00:39:57.740 align:middle line:84%
So I want to look
at player i, and I

00:39:57.740 --> 00:40:01.180 align:middle line:84%
want to say if player i's
strategy has survived up

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:04.260 align:middle line:84%
to round k, then player i
strategies should survive up

00:40:04.260 --> 00:40:07.020 align:middle line:90%
to round k plus 1.

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:09.260 align:middle line:84%
That's not going to
get you very far.

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:12.980 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is that which
strategies for player i

00:40:12.980 --> 00:40:15.260 align:middle line:84%
survive up to round
k plus 1 depend

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:17.220 align:middle line:84%
on which strategies of
player i's opponents

00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:20.433 align:middle line:84%
have survived up to round k
because, when I'm choosing,

00:40:20.433 --> 00:40:22.100 align:middle line:84%
when we go through
the algorithm, what's

00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:23.980 align:middle line:90%
the best response for player i?

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:27.540 align:middle line:84%
It depends on what
strategies of my opponents

00:40:27.540 --> 00:40:28.860 align:middle line:90%
have survived so far.

00:40:28.860 --> 00:40:31.043 align:middle line:84%
So we really have to
reason simultaneously

00:40:31.043 --> 00:40:31.960 align:middle line:90%
about all the players.

00:40:31.960 --> 00:40:33.710 align:middle line:84%
We can't just reason
through it separately

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:36.500 align:middle line:84%
because which strategies
survive for player i depend

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:40.060 align:middle line:84%
on which strategies of the other
players have survived so far.

00:40:40.060 --> 00:40:43.980 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to go
through this argument.

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:54.880 align:middle line:90%


00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:57.160 align:middle line:90%
OK, so here's the argument.

00:40:57.160 --> 00:40:59.020 align:middle line:90%
So let's consider player i.

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:05.120 align:middle line:90%


00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:08.200 align:middle line:84%
So we know that si
star is in si k.

00:41:08.200 --> 00:41:10.920 align:middle line:84%
But I've argued that that's
actually not so useful for us.

00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:14.920 align:middle line:84%
What's more important from
the perspective of player i?

00:41:14.920 --> 00:41:16.960 align:middle line:84%
What do I care about
as I'm player i?

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:19.920 align:middle line:84%
I care about what strategies
my opponents might play.

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:26.240 align:middle line:84%
So the important thing is
that we know that s negative i

00:41:26.240 --> 00:41:30.600 align:middle line:90%
star is in s negative i k.

00:41:30.600 --> 00:41:34.480 align:middle line:84%
This is the key part of our--
so our inductive assumption

00:41:34.480 --> 00:41:38.680 align:middle line:84%
is that everyone's strategy
has survived so far?

00:41:38.680 --> 00:41:41.140 align:middle line:84%
But the relevant aspect
of that assumption

00:41:41.140 --> 00:41:43.220 align:middle line:84%
is the fact that all of
my opponent strategies

00:41:43.220 --> 00:41:44.780 align:middle line:84%
have survived so
far because that's

00:41:44.780 --> 00:41:46.860 align:middle line:84%
what's going to
determine what strategies

00:41:46.860 --> 00:41:48.500 align:middle line:90%
are best responses for me.

00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:49.640 align:middle line:90%
So this is what we know.

00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:52.380 align:middle line:90%


00:41:52.380 --> 00:42:05.520 align:middle line:84%
And what we want to show is
that si star is in si k plus 1.

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:07.872 align:middle line:90%
So this is what we know.

00:42:07.872 --> 00:42:09.080 align:middle line:90%
This is what we want to show.

00:42:09.080 --> 00:42:11.663 align:middle line:84%
I think this is often a good way
to try to get through proofs.

00:42:11.663 --> 00:42:13.288 align:middle line:84%
So I think the first
thing we should do

00:42:13.288 --> 00:42:14.800 align:middle line:84%
is just write down
some definitions.

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:19.260 align:middle line:90%
What is si k plus 1?

00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:21.520 align:middle line:84%
What is the definition
of si k plus 1?

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:29.260 align:middle line:90%


00:42:29.260 --> 00:42:30.860 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, over here.

00:42:30.860 --> 00:42:34.980 align:middle line:90%
In the Boston sweatshirt, yeah.

00:42:34.980 --> 00:42:38.040 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's like all--

00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:45.000 align:middle line:84%
it's the set of strategies that
are a best response to something

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:46.860 align:middle line:90%
in si k.

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:47.860 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:42:47.860 --> 00:42:49.060 align:middle line:90%
And we have a notation for that.

00:42:49.060 --> 00:42:50.518 align:middle line:84%
That's exactly what
it is in words.

00:42:50.518 --> 00:42:53.080 align:middle line:84%
And in math-- we'll just
convert that into math,

00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:56.600 align:middle line:90%
is s negative i k.

00:42:56.600 --> 00:43:01.280 align:middle line:84%
So which strategies survive
up to the k plus first round?

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:03.840 align:middle line:84%
It's all the
strategies for player i

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:07.120 align:middle line:84%
that are best
responses for player i

00:43:07.120 --> 00:43:11.880 align:middle line:84%
to some belief over the
strategies in s negative i k?

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:14.200 align:middle line:84%
So these are the strategies
I consider it possible

00:43:14.200 --> 00:43:16.020 align:middle line:90%
that my opponents might play.

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:20.520 align:middle line:84%
And I want to think about
all best responses to that.

00:43:20.520 --> 00:43:22.720 align:middle line:84%
So now we just have to
put things together.

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:27.120 align:middle line:84%
We know that s negative i
star is in s negative i k.

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:30.800 align:middle line:84%
We want to show that si star
is a best response to something

00:43:30.800 --> 00:43:32.920 align:middle line:90%
in s negative i k.

00:43:32.920 --> 00:43:35.420 align:middle line:90%
What's the key step?

00:43:35.420 --> 00:43:36.320 align:middle line:90%
What's the leap here?

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:38.320 align:middle line:90%
So why is this true?

00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:44.140 align:middle line:90%


00:43:44.140 --> 00:43:44.860 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:44.860 --> 00:43:47.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So si star is by
definition a best response

00:43:47.900 --> 00:43:50.340 align:middle line:84%
to s negative i star,
and that's what we just

00:43:50.340 --> 00:43:52.398 align:middle line:84%
showed because their
game best [INAUDIBLE].

00:43:52.398 --> 00:43:53.440 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly, right.

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:55.260 align:middle line:84%
So by the definition
of Nash equilibrium--

00:43:55.260 --> 00:43:56.420 align:middle line:90%
let me explain this.

00:43:56.420 --> 00:43:58.580 align:middle line:84%
By the definition
of Nash equilibrium,

00:43:58.580 --> 00:44:02.380 align:middle line:84%
we know that si star is a best
response to s negative i star.

00:44:02.380 --> 00:44:10.180 align:middle line:90%


00:44:10.180 --> 00:44:12.760 align:middle line:84%
And remember, this is a
small s, this is a big S,

00:44:12.760 --> 00:44:14.180 align:middle line:90%
this is a small s.

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:16.740 align:middle line:84%
So we know that
si star is a best

00:44:16.740 --> 00:44:19.660 align:middle line:90%
response to s negative i star.

00:44:19.660 --> 00:44:21.940 align:middle line:84%
And we know by our
inductive hypothesis

00:44:21.940 --> 00:44:26.020 align:middle line:84%
that s star is a strategy
in s negative i k.

00:44:26.020 --> 00:44:28.500 align:middle line:84%
So it just follows by
definition that, therefore,

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:31.540 align:middle line:84%
s negative i star is a best
response to some belief

00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:33.120 align:middle line:90%
about things in s negative i k.

00:44:33.120 --> 00:44:34.720 align:middle line:84%
Specifically, it's
a best response

00:44:34.720 --> 00:44:38.360 align:middle line:84%
to the belief that puts all the
probability on this strategy.

00:44:38.360 --> 00:44:39.680 align:middle line:90%
So we're done.

00:44:39.680 --> 00:44:44.400 align:middle line:84%
We've shown that si
star is in si k plus 1.

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:47.140 align:middle line:84%
And if we reason exactly the
same way for all the players,

00:44:47.140 --> 00:44:49.520 align:middle line:84%
we'll show that this holds
for every single player i,

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:51.340 align:middle line:84%
and now we've completed
our inductive step.

00:44:51.340 --> 00:44:56.640 align:middle line:90%


00:44:56.640 --> 00:44:59.420 align:middle line:84%
So again, I think each
individual step is not too hard.

00:44:59.420 --> 00:45:01.680 align:middle line:84%
But piecing it together
and thinking through this

00:45:01.680 --> 00:45:02.460 align:middle line:90%
is a bit tricky.

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:05.380 align:middle line:84%
So again, are there any
questions on this argument?

00:45:05.380 --> 00:45:11.000 align:middle line:90%


00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:14.660 align:middle line:84%
So now let's go
a little farther.

00:45:14.660 --> 00:45:16.260 align:middle line:90%
Let's go to another example.

00:45:16.260 --> 00:45:31.237 align:middle line:90%


00:45:31.237 --> 00:45:32.820 align:middle line:84%
This is another
classic game, and this

00:45:32.820 --> 00:45:37.060 align:middle line:90%
is called hide-and-seek.

00:45:37.060 --> 00:45:39.080 align:middle line:84%
So think of this as
the childhood game.

00:45:39.080 --> 00:45:40.663 align:middle line:84%
Someone hides, the
other person seeks,

00:45:40.663 --> 00:45:42.372 align:middle line:84%
and the hider doesn't
want to get caught,

00:45:42.372 --> 00:45:44.660 align:middle line:84%
and the seeker does want
to catch the other person.

00:45:44.660 --> 00:45:46.715 align:middle line:84%
Of course, there's more
substantive examples.

00:45:46.715 --> 00:45:48.840 align:middle line:84%
You might think of someone
cheating on their taxes,

00:45:48.840 --> 00:45:50.548 align:middle line:84%
trying to hide, and
then the IRS seeking,

00:45:50.548 --> 00:45:53.173 align:middle line:84%
trying to figure out which line
of their taxes they cheated on.

00:45:53.173 --> 00:45:55.200 align:middle line:84%
So I think it has
broader implications,

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:57.120 align:middle line:84%
but we'll keep with the
hide and seek story.

00:45:57.120 --> 00:45:58.940 align:middle line:84%
So let's say player
1 is the hider,

00:45:58.940 --> 00:46:01.260 align:middle line:84%
and there's two
places they can hide.

00:46:01.260 --> 00:46:03.380 align:middle line:84%
And player 2 is the seeker,
and there's two places

00:46:03.380 --> 00:46:05.700 align:middle line:90%
they can look for the person.

00:46:05.700 --> 00:46:10.980 align:middle line:84%
So the seeker is going to win if
they look where the person hid.

00:46:10.980 --> 00:46:12.940 align:middle line:84%
So that means we're
going to have--

00:46:12.940 --> 00:46:15.300 align:middle line:84%
give myself a
little more space--

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:18.340 align:middle line:90%
negative 1, 1, negative 1, 1.

00:46:18.340 --> 00:46:22.260 align:middle line:84%
Because here, person
1 hid in spot A,

00:46:22.260 --> 00:46:24.500 align:middle line:84%
person 2 looked
for them in spot A.

00:46:24.500 --> 00:46:26.540 align:middle line:84%
That means person 1
gets caught, so they

00:46:26.540 --> 00:46:28.540 align:middle line:84%
get a payoff of negative
1, and the other player

00:46:28.540 --> 00:46:30.080 align:middle line:90%
gets a payoff of 1.

00:46:30.080 --> 00:46:34.560 align:middle line:84%
And the same is true if they
both hide and seek in spot B.

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:37.840 align:middle line:84%
But then, over here, we're
going to have the opposite.

00:46:37.840 --> 00:46:41.680 align:middle line:84%
So here, if i, as player 1,
hide in spot B and player 2

00:46:41.680 --> 00:46:44.000 align:middle line:84%
looks for me in A, then
they're not going to find me.

00:46:44.000 --> 00:46:45.618 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I
get a payoff of 1

00:46:45.618 --> 00:46:47.660 align:middle line:84%
and the other player gets
a payoff of negative 1.

00:46:47.660 --> 00:46:50.280 align:middle line:84%
You could also think as the
hider as the penalty taker

00:46:50.280 --> 00:46:52.000 align:middle line:90%
and the seeker as the goalie.

00:46:52.000 --> 00:46:54.083 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of different
stories you could tell.

00:46:54.083 --> 00:46:56.152 align:middle line:90%


00:46:56.152 --> 00:46:58.360 align:middle line:84%
So notice a difference
between this game and the game

00:46:58.360 --> 00:46:59.440 align:middle line:90%
we discussed before.

00:46:59.440 --> 00:47:03.300 align:middle line:84%
So this is a game of pure
conflict of interest.

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:10.240 align:middle line:90%


00:47:10.240 --> 00:47:12.502 align:middle line:84%
In the Boston game,
there were both.

00:47:12.502 --> 00:47:13.960 align:middle line:84%
There was some
conflict of interest

00:47:13.960 --> 00:47:16.085 align:middle line:84%
because one person liked
the Red Sox and one person

00:47:16.085 --> 00:47:16.980 align:middle line:90%
liked the Celtics.

00:47:16.980 --> 00:47:18.440 align:middle line:84%
But there was also
some common interest

00:47:18.440 --> 00:47:19.920 align:middle line:84%
because they both liked
each other's company,

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:21.560 align:middle line:90%
and they wanted to be together.

00:47:21.560 --> 00:47:23.600 align:middle line:84%
Here, it's purely a
conflict of interest.

00:47:23.600 --> 00:47:25.180 align:middle line:90%
I want us as player 1.

00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:28.060 align:middle line:84%
I want us to hide
in different places.

00:47:28.060 --> 00:47:29.560 align:middle line:90%
So maybe I'll say that.

00:47:29.560 --> 00:47:33.100 align:middle line:90%
So what does player 1 want?

00:47:33.100 --> 00:47:36.420 align:middle line:84%
They want them to look and
hide in different places, maybe

00:47:36.420 --> 00:47:37.560 align:middle line:90%
different locations.

00:47:37.560 --> 00:47:40.260 align:middle line:90%


00:47:40.260 --> 00:47:43.600 align:middle line:84%
And player 2 wants them to
be in the same location.

00:47:43.600 --> 00:47:47.385 align:middle line:90%


00:47:47.385 --> 00:47:49.760 align:middle line:84%
Of course, the part is you
can only choose your location.

00:47:49.760 --> 00:47:53.940 align:middle line:84%
If you chose the other person's
location, then it would be easy.

00:47:53.940 --> 00:47:55.780 align:middle line:84%
So as a first step
for this, let's go

00:47:55.780 --> 00:47:59.980 align:middle line:84%
through our standard procedure
where we compute player 1's best

00:47:59.980 --> 00:48:04.060 align:middle line:84%
responses to player 2 and player
2's best responses to player 1.

00:48:04.060 --> 00:48:05.680 align:middle line:90%
So let's do it slowly.

00:48:05.680 --> 00:48:11.000 align:middle line:84%
If I'm player 1, if player 2
guesses A, then I want to hide.

00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:15.340 align:middle line:84%
So I want to do B. And
if player 2 guesses B,

00:48:15.340 --> 00:48:17.560 align:middle line:84%
I want to hide and be
in a different place.

00:48:17.560 --> 00:48:20.340 align:middle line:90%
So I would like to be A.

00:48:20.340 --> 00:48:21.900 align:middle line:84%
Now let's look at
it the other way.

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:25.960 align:middle line:84%
Now let's look at the
perspective of player 2.

00:48:25.960 --> 00:48:30.600 align:middle line:84%
If player 1 plays A, then player
2 does better if they also go A

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:32.280 align:middle line:84%
because they want
to find the person.

00:48:32.280 --> 00:48:39.060 align:middle line:84%
And if player 1 goes B, then
player two wants to go B.

00:48:39.060 --> 00:48:41.960 align:middle line:84%
So we've computed
the best responses.

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:45.080 align:middle line:84%
We can look, and if we
follow our algorithm,

00:48:45.080 --> 00:48:47.680 align:middle line:84%
where are the Nash
equilibria in this game?

00:48:47.680 --> 00:48:51.260 align:middle line:84%
We want to look for a cell where
both numbers are underlined.

00:48:51.260 --> 00:48:54.040 align:middle line:84%
But here, we have a problem
because none of the cells

00:48:54.040 --> 00:48:55.800 align:middle line:90%
are underlined.

00:48:55.800 --> 00:48:59.458 align:middle line:84%
So what this tells us is
that this game has no--

00:48:59.458 --> 00:49:02.000 align:middle line:84%
and this will be important in
the second-- pure-strategy Nash

00:49:02.000 --> 00:49:02.780 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:49:02.780 --> 00:49:07.040 align:middle line:90%


00:49:07.040 --> 00:49:09.100 align:middle line:84%
And we can think through
it reasonably right.

00:49:09.100 --> 00:49:12.320 align:middle line:90%


00:49:12.320 --> 00:49:14.720 align:middle line:84%
In order for us to have
a Nash equilibrium,

00:49:14.720 --> 00:49:17.560 align:middle line:84%
neither player can have
a profitable deviation.

00:49:17.560 --> 00:49:20.680 align:middle line:84%
But if we're in the same
place, then someone certainly

00:49:20.680 --> 00:49:22.640 align:middle line:90%
has a profitable deviation.

00:49:22.640 --> 00:49:26.320 align:middle line:84%
The hider would rather deviate
if we're in the same place.

00:49:26.320 --> 00:49:27.695 align:middle line:84%
And if we're in
different places,

00:49:27.695 --> 00:49:29.487 align:middle line:84%
then, again, someone
would like to deviate,

00:49:29.487 --> 00:49:31.780 align:middle line:84%
the seeker would like to
deviate and change places

00:49:31.780 --> 00:49:34.180 align:middle line:84%
so that they find the
person who's hiding.

00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:36.400 align:middle line:84%
So because of this immense
conflict of interest,

00:49:36.400 --> 00:49:38.600 align:middle line:84%
we can't have a pure-strategy
Nash equilibrium

00:49:38.600 --> 00:49:41.083 align:middle line:84%
because someone's losing,
and whoever's losing

00:49:41.083 --> 00:49:43.500 align:middle line:84%
would rather change their
behavior so that they could win.

00:49:43.500 --> 00:49:45.820 align:middle line:84%
This is a zero-sum
game in a formal sense

00:49:45.820 --> 00:49:47.673 align:middle line:84%
that we'll talk
about more later.

00:49:47.673 --> 00:49:49.340 align:middle line:84%
But we'd still like
to make a prediction

00:49:49.340 --> 00:49:50.660 align:middle line:90%
about play in this game.

00:49:50.660 --> 00:49:52.558 align:middle line:84%
If we just use
rationalizability,

00:49:52.558 --> 00:49:54.100 align:middle line:84%
well, anything could
happen because I

00:49:54.100 --> 00:49:56.240 align:middle line:84%
can hide an A if I think the
other person's going to B,

00:49:56.240 --> 00:49:57.660 align:middle line:84%
and I can hide in B If I
think they're going to A,

00:49:57.660 --> 00:49:59.540 align:middle line:90%
and we can explain everything.

00:49:59.540 --> 00:50:01.700 align:middle line:84%
So I don't know, how
would you play this game?

00:50:01.700 --> 00:50:04.860 align:middle line:84%
If you were taking penalty
kicks in the World Cup,

00:50:04.860 --> 00:50:08.400 align:middle line:84%
how would you-- what
strategy would you use?

00:50:08.400 --> 00:50:08.900 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:50:08.900 --> 00:50:09.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Mixed-strategy.

00:50:09.680 --> 00:50:11.513 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: You'd use a
mixed strategy, right?

00:50:11.513 --> 00:50:12.320 align:middle line:90%
So you'd randomize.

00:50:12.320 --> 00:50:15.120 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, how do you
think you would randomize?

00:50:15.120 --> 00:50:17.900 align:middle line:90%


00:50:17.900 --> 00:50:21.043 align:middle line:84%
Would you play A
99% of the time?

00:50:21.043 --> 00:50:21.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 1/2 and 1/2.

00:50:21.960 --> 00:50:24.543 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Probably 1/2 and 1/2
because you don't want yourself

00:50:24.543 --> 00:50:25.520 align:middle line:90%
to be predictable.

00:50:25.520 --> 00:50:27.820 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the data on
World Cup penalty takers,

00:50:27.820 --> 00:50:29.153 align:middle line:90%
that's a pretty good prediction.

00:50:29.153 --> 00:50:31.128 align:middle line:84%
I mean, sometimes
right-footed players

00:50:31.128 --> 00:50:32.920 align:middle line:84%
have a bit of a left
advantage, and there's

00:50:32.920 --> 00:50:33.860 align:middle line:90%
all these subtleties.

00:50:33.860 --> 00:50:37.240 align:middle line:84%
But roughly, people mix
50/50 because if I develop

00:50:37.240 --> 00:50:39.320 align:middle line:84%
a reputation, if
everyone knows that--

00:50:39.320 --> 00:50:41.777 align:middle line:84%
if you think about playing
hide and seek with a young kid,

00:50:41.777 --> 00:50:43.860 align:middle line:84%
sometimes they just always
hide in the same place.

00:50:43.860 --> 00:50:45.520 align:middle line:90%
It's pretty easy to beat them.

00:50:45.520 --> 00:50:47.820 align:middle line:84%
And even if you don't always
hide in the same place,

00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:49.740 align:middle line:84%
if you hid in the same
place 95% of the time,

00:50:49.740 --> 00:50:51.380 align:middle line:84%
you'd also be
pretty easy to find.

00:50:51.380 --> 00:50:53.902 align:middle line:84%
So it seems pretty intuitive
that you'd want to do 1/2, 1/2.

00:50:53.902 --> 00:50:54.860 align:middle line:90%
So that's a good guess.

00:50:54.860 --> 00:50:55.780 align:middle line:90%
And that's right.

00:50:55.780 --> 00:50:59.480 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out it will have a
mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium.

00:50:59.480 --> 00:51:06.638 align:middle line:84%
But 1/2, 1/2, meaning
player 1 randomizes 1/2,

00:51:06.638 --> 00:51:08.680 align:middle line:84%
1/2 between A and B, let
me write this a bit more

00:51:08.680 --> 00:51:09.920 align:middle line:90%
explicitly.

00:51:09.920 --> 00:51:16.080 align:middle line:84%
1/2 A plus 1/2 B comma
1/2 A plus 1/2 B.

00:51:16.080 --> 00:51:21.210 align:middle line:84%
This means player 1 mixes
50/50 and player 2 mixes 50/50.

00:51:21.210 --> 00:51:24.310 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be a Nash
equilibrium in mixed strategies.

00:51:24.310 --> 00:51:26.310 align:middle line:90%
So I'd say this is--

00:51:26.310 --> 00:51:28.850 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll use the
acronym Mixed-Strategy.

00:51:28.850 --> 00:51:32.703 align:middle line:84%
NE, MSNE, mixed-strategy
Nash equilibrium.

00:51:32.703 --> 00:51:34.370 align:middle line:84%
But I haven't even
defined what that is.

00:51:34.370 --> 00:51:35.947 align:middle line:90%
So let's now define that.

00:51:35.947 --> 00:51:37.530 align:middle line:84%
I think it's exactly
what you'd think.

00:51:37.530 --> 00:52:01.870 align:middle line:90%


00:52:01.870 --> 00:52:06.050 align:middle line:84%
So definition, a
mixed-strategy profile--

00:52:06.050 --> 00:52:12.350 align:middle line:90%


00:52:12.350 --> 00:52:14.070 align:middle line:84%
and now we'll use
sigma instead of s

00:52:14.070 --> 00:52:15.650 align:middle line:90%
to remind us that this is mixed.

00:52:15.650 --> 00:52:20.330 align:middle line:84%
So sigma star
equals sigma 1 star,

00:52:20.330 --> 00:52:26.310 align:middle line:84%
sigma n star is a
Nash equilibrium--

00:52:26.310 --> 00:52:31.570 align:middle line:90%


00:52:31.570 --> 00:52:42.690 align:middle line:84%
and we'll give exactly the same
definition, for each player i,

00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:43.710 align:middle line:90%
what happens?

00:52:43.710 --> 00:52:51.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, I'm comparing now what
I'm supposed to do as player i

00:52:51.530 --> 00:52:57.890 align:middle line:84%
to a deviation, which I'll
maybe call sigma i prime.

00:52:57.890 --> 00:53:00.770 align:middle line:84%
So this is the mixed-strategy
I'm actually playing.

00:53:00.770 --> 00:53:03.410 align:middle line:84%
This is the mixed-strategy
I could deviate to.

00:53:03.410 --> 00:53:06.490 align:middle line:84%
And I want this to be optimal
given the way my opponents are

00:53:06.490 --> 00:53:07.030 align:middle line:90%
playing.

00:53:07.030 --> 00:53:16.170 align:middle line:90%


00:53:16.170 --> 00:53:17.870 align:middle line:90%
So this is some new notation.

00:53:17.870 --> 00:53:21.270 align:middle line:84%
We've been using capital SI to
denote all the pure strategies

00:53:21.270 --> 00:53:22.350 align:middle line:90%
of player i.

00:53:22.350 --> 00:53:25.532 align:middle line:84%
It's often convenient to use,
this, it's the summation symbol,

00:53:25.532 --> 00:53:26.990 align:middle line:84%
and this is capital
Sigma, and this

00:53:26.990 --> 00:53:29.130 align:middle line:84%
is the set of all mixed
strategies by player i.

00:53:29.130 --> 00:53:35.070 align:middle line:90%


00:53:35.070 --> 00:53:37.510 align:middle line:84%
So we use lowercase
s for pure strategy,

00:53:37.510 --> 00:53:40.510 align:middle line:84%
uppercase S for a set
of pure strategies,

00:53:40.510 --> 00:53:43.150 align:middle line:84%
lowercase sigma for
a mixed strategy,

00:53:43.150 --> 00:53:47.710 align:middle line:84%
and uppercase Sigma for a
set of mixed strategies.

00:53:47.710 --> 00:53:52.070 align:middle line:84%
So it's the same idea, given the
way my opponents are playing,

00:53:52.070 --> 00:53:53.850 align:middle line:90%
my strategy is a best response.

00:53:53.850 --> 00:53:55.950 align:middle line:84%
My mixed strategy
does weakly better

00:53:55.950 --> 00:53:58.390 align:middle line:84%
than any other mixed
strategy I could use.

00:53:58.390 --> 00:53:59.970 align:middle line:84%
But if you look at
this definition,

00:53:59.970 --> 00:54:03.190 align:middle line:84%
it's pretty messy because
there's so many different mixed

00:54:03.190 --> 00:54:05.570 align:middle line:90%
strategies I could deviate to.

00:54:05.570 --> 00:54:07.810 align:middle line:84%
Even in a game with
just two strategies,

00:54:07.810 --> 00:54:11.070 align:middle line:84%
I could deviate to any
probability between 0 and 1.

00:54:11.070 --> 00:54:12.853 align:middle line:84%
That's a lot of
deviations to deal with.

00:54:12.853 --> 00:54:14.270 align:middle line:84%
But fortunately,
we don't actually

00:54:14.270 --> 00:54:15.570 align:middle line:90%
have to worry about that.

00:54:15.570 --> 00:54:19.590 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out, even if I were
to check every mixed strategy,

00:54:19.590 --> 00:54:22.370 align:middle line:84%
it's actually enough just
to check my pure strategy

00:54:22.370 --> 00:54:23.690 align:middle line:90%
deviations.

00:54:23.690 --> 00:54:27.450 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out this
is the same as--

00:54:27.450 --> 00:54:34.410 align:middle line:84%
the same thing, but with
si prime for all si prime.

00:54:34.410 --> 00:54:36.290 align:middle line:84%
So when I use the
two dashes, I'm

00:54:36.290 --> 00:54:38.783 align:middle line:84%
just saying everything else
in the definition is the same.

00:54:38.783 --> 00:54:40.450 align:middle line:84%
But instead of saying,
it must do better

00:54:40.450 --> 00:54:42.370 align:middle line:84%
than any mixed
strategy, I'm saying

00:54:42.370 --> 00:54:45.970 align:middle line:84%
it must do weakly better
than any pure strategy.

00:54:45.970 --> 00:54:48.030 align:middle line:84%
And we could give a
formal math proof,

00:54:48.030 --> 00:54:50.530 align:middle line:84%
but let's just think
of the intuition.

00:54:50.530 --> 00:54:53.970 align:middle line:84%
If I'd rather do what
I'm doing than deviate

00:54:53.970 --> 00:54:56.370 align:middle line:84%
to any pure strategy,
then by choosing

00:54:56.370 --> 00:54:58.790 align:middle line:84%
to flip a coin and mix
over those pure strategies,

00:54:58.790 --> 00:55:01.210 align:middle line:84%
I can't do strictly better
because if I mix over

00:55:01.210 --> 00:55:03.330 align:middle line:84%
those pure strategies,
my payoff is just

00:55:03.330 --> 00:55:05.610 align:middle line:84%
going to be an
average of my payoff

00:55:05.610 --> 00:55:07.530 align:middle line:90%
from all the pure deviations.

00:55:07.530 --> 00:55:08.970 align:middle line:84%
So if each of the
pure deviations

00:55:08.970 --> 00:55:11.690 align:middle line:84%
makes me weakly worse off,
then a mixture of them

00:55:11.690 --> 00:55:13.330 align:middle line:84%
must also make me
weakly worse off.

00:55:13.330 --> 00:55:14.348 align:middle line:90%
That's the intuition.

00:55:14.348 --> 00:55:16.390 align:middle line:84%
You could write down a
formal mathematical proof.

00:55:16.390 --> 00:55:19.710 align:middle line:84%
But the point is, it's
really important here

00:55:19.710 --> 00:55:22.130 align:middle line:84%
that what I am
doing can be mixed.

00:55:22.130 --> 00:55:24.010 align:middle line:84%
So I want people to
really understand this.

00:55:24.010 --> 00:55:24.810 align:middle line:90%
This is crucial.

00:55:24.810 --> 00:55:27.510 align:middle line:90%


00:55:27.510 --> 00:55:30.430 align:middle line:84%
It's crucial that we're
allowing my equilibrium strategy

00:55:30.430 --> 00:55:32.350 align:middle line:90%
to be mixed and not pure.

00:55:32.350 --> 00:55:34.930 align:middle line:84%
But when I contemplate
the deviations,

00:55:34.930 --> 00:55:37.330 align:middle line:84%
it's enough to only
look at pure strategies.

00:55:37.330 --> 00:55:39.850 align:middle line:84%
And that's a key
difference here.

00:55:39.850 --> 00:55:42.612 align:middle line:84%
So indeed, how can we
tell that mixtures matter?

00:55:42.612 --> 00:55:44.070 align:middle line:84%
Well, already in
this game here, we

00:55:44.070 --> 00:55:47.090 align:middle line:84%
saw that it didn't have a
pure-strategy Nash equilibrium.

00:55:47.090 --> 00:55:49.290 align:middle line:84%
But it does have a
mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium.

00:55:49.290 --> 00:55:52.510 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that we allow the
equilibrium strategy to be mixed

00:55:52.510 --> 00:55:53.830 align:middle line:90%
is essential.

00:55:53.830 --> 00:55:55.950 align:middle line:84%
But when you're
contemplating deviations,

00:55:55.950 --> 00:55:59.070 align:middle line:84%
you only have to worry
about pure strategies.

00:55:59.070 --> 00:56:00.670 align:middle line:84%
And let's take
that randomization

00:56:00.670 --> 00:56:01.570 align:middle line:90%
idea little further.

00:56:01.570 --> 00:56:03.930 align:middle line:84%
Why would I ever want to
play a mixed strategy?

00:56:03.930 --> 00:56:07.070 align:middle line:84%
I mean, why am I willing to flip
a coin and sometimes go to A

00:56:07.070 --> 00:56:08.425 align:middle line:90%
and sometimes go to B?

00:56:08.425 --> 00:56:11.050 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if A is better, wouldn't
I just always want to go to A?

00:56:11.050 --> 00:56:13.470 align:middle line:84%
And if B is better, wouldn't
I just always want to go to B?

00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:15.012 align:middle line:84%
Why would I ever
want to flip a coin?

00:56:15.012 --> 00:56:17.090 align:middle line:84%
Or why would I ever be
willing to flip a coin

00:56:17.090 --> 00:56:21.570 align:middle line:84%
and play A some of the time
and B some of the time?

00:56:21.570 --> 00:56:22.590 align:middle line:90%
What must be true?

00:56:22.590 --> 00:56:27.410 align:middle line:90%


00:56:27.410 --> 00:56:28.218 align:middle line:90%
If I'm-- yeah.

00:56:28.218 --> 00:56:30.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: A is better
in some circumstances

00:56:30.010 --> 00:56:31.352 align:middle line:90%
while B is better [INAUDIBLE].

00:56:31.352 --> 00:56:32.810 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: OK, that's
true, but what

00:56:32.810 --> 00:56:35.393 align:middle line:84%
about given my belief, given the
way my opponents are playing,

00:56:35.393 --> 00:56:36.230 align:middle line:90%
what must be true?

00:56:36.230 --> 00:56:39.370 align:middle line:90%


00:56:39.370 --> 00:56:41.730 align:middle line:84%
If I strictly
preferred A to B, given

00:56:41.730 --> 00:56:43.250 align:middle line:84%
the way my opponents
were playing,

00:56:43.250 --> 00:56:45.490 align:middle line:84%
would I want to mix
between A and B?

00:56:45.490 --> 00:56:46.010 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:56:46.010 --> 00:56:46.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:56:46.550 --> 00:56:48.133 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does your
opponent also have

00:56:48.133 --> 00:56:49.730 align:middle line:90%
to be playing a mixed strategy?

00:56:49.730 --> 00:56:52.830 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So that's related, but
maybe not quite where we are.

00:56:52.830 --> 00:56:54.750 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm willing to
mix between A and B,

00:56:54.750 --> 00:56:57.250 align:middle line:84%
it must be that I'm
indifferent between A and B.

00:56:57.250 --> 00:56:58.430 align:middle line:90%
That's the key thing.

00:56:58.430 --> 00:57:00.890 align:middle line:84%
If I strictly
preferred A to B, I

00:57:00.890 --> 00:57:03.410 align:middle line:84%
would never play B with
positive probability at all.

00:57:03.410 --> 00:57:05.690 align:middle line:84%
And if I strictly
preferred B, I would never

00:57:05.690 --> 00:57:07.470 align:middle line:84%
play A with positive
probability?

00:57:07.470 --> 00:57:09.590 align:middle line:84%
So I'm only willing
to flip a coin

00:57:09.590 --> 00:57:12.983 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes play A and
sometimes play B if I'm exactly

00:57:12.983 --> 00:57:14.150 align:middle line:90%
indifferent between A and B.

00:57:14.150 --> 00:57:15.250 align:middle line:90%
Now this gets to your point.

00:57:15.250 --> 00:57:17.050 align:middle line:84%
Why will I be indifferent
between A and B?

00:57:17.050 --> 00:57:21.150 align:middle line:84%
It'll be precisely because
my opponent is mixing.

00:57:21.150 --> 00:57:23.490 align:middle line:90%
So this is a key observation.

00:57:23.490 --> 00:57:28.470 align:middle line:90%


00:57:28.470 --> 00:57:30.170 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll start a new board.

00:57:30.170 --> 00:57:38.150 align:middle line:90%


00:57:38.150 --> 00:57:40.330 align:middle line:90%
So this is the key observation.

00:57:40.330 --> 00:57:47.630 align:middle line:90%


00:57:47.630 --> 00:57:51.790 align:middle line:90%
So mixing is optimal.

00:57:51.790 --> 00:57:54.190 align:middle line:84%
Only if you're indifferent
between the things

00:57:54.190 --> 00:57:55.090 align:middle line:90%
you're mixing over.

00:57:55.090 --> 00:58:03.990 align:middle line:90%


00:58:03.990 --> 00:58:07.080 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, we can say
something even stronger.

00:58:07.080 --> 00:58:08.830 align:middle line:84%
It's not just enough
that I'm indifferent,

00:58:08.830 --> 00:58:11.530 align:middle line:84%
but I'm only willing to mix
over pure strategies that

00:58:11.530 --> 00:58:13.610 align:middle line:90%
are best responses.

00:58:13.610 --> 00:58:15.690 align:middle line:90%
So it must be--

00:58:15.690 --> 00:58:28.030 align:middle line:84%
and in fact, it's only optimal
to mix over pure best responses.

00:58:28.030 --> 00:58:35.192 align:middle line:90%


00:58:35.192 --> 00:58:36.150 align:middle line:90%
So what does that mean?

00:58:36.150 --> 00:58:38.010 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's just a silly example.

00:58:38.010 --> 00:58:41.210 align:middle line:84%
What if I had A,
B, and C and given

00:58:41.210 --> 00:58:44.250 align:middle line:84%
the way my opponents were
playing, A gave me 1,

00:58:44.250 --> 00:58:47.410 align:middle line:90%
B gave me 1, and C gave me 2.

00:58:47.410 --> 00:58:49.080 align:middle line:90%
Do I want to mix over A and B?

00:58:49.080 --> 00:58:51.330 align:middle line:84%
Well, no, even though I'm
indifferent between A and B,

00:58:51.330 --> 00:58:52.830 align:middle line:84%
that's necessary, but
it's not sufficient.

00:58:52.830 --> 00:58:54.580 align:middle line:84%
I certainly don't want
to mix over A and B

00:58:54.580 --> 00:58:55.810 align:middle line:90%
because C is strictly better.

00:58:55.810 --> 00:58:57.590 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm willing to mix,
I must be indifferent.

00:58:57.590 --> 00:58:58.530 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes
people get confused,

00:58:58.530 --> 00:59:00.590 align:middle line:84%
and they get it backwards and
they say, if I'm indifferent,

00:59:00.590 --> 00:59:01.752 align:middle line:90%
I must be willing to mix.

00:59:01.752 --> 00:59:03.710 align:middle line:84%
But no, it's not just
enough to be indifferent.

00:59:03.710 --> 00:59:06.030 align:middle line:84%
It also has to be optimal
what you're mixing over.

00:59:06.030 --> 00:59:08.090 align:middle line:84%
And that's precisely
what I'm saying here.

00:59:08.090 --> 00:59:10.690 align:middle line:84%
It's only optimal to mix
over pure best responses.

00:59:10.690 --> 00:59:14.270 align:middle line:84%
So if I added D at
2, now, indeed, I'd

00:59:14.270 --> 00:59:20.110 align:middle line:84%
be willing to mix
between C and D.

00:59:20.110 --> 00:59:22.790 align:middle line:90%
So now let's try to check--

00:59:22.790 --> 00:59:25.070 align:middle line:90%
going back to this game--

00:59:25.070 --> 00:59:29.870 align:middle line:84%
that 1/2, 1/2 is actually
a Nash equilibrium.

00:59:29.870 --> 00:59:31.730 align:middle line:84%
So I think the best
way to do it is to say,

00:59:31.730 --> 00:59:34.790 align:middle line:84%
well, let's take the
perspective of player 2.

00:59:34.790 --> 00:59:38.470 align:middle line:84%
And let's suppose that
player 1 mixes 1/2, 1/2.

00:59:38.470 --> 00:59:41.525 align:middle line:84%
What payoff does player 2 get
from playing A and playing B?

00:59:41.525 --> 00:59:44.150 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to imagine is
I'm going to add a new strategy

00:59:44.150 --> 00:59:50.670 align:middle line:84%
here, 1/2 A, 1/2 B. So I'm
adding a new row to this matrix

00:59:50.670 --> 00:59:54.150 align:middle line:84%
that's going to represent the
payoffs if player 1 chooses this

00:59:54.150 --> 00:59:55.610 align:middle line:90%
mixed strategy.

00:59:55.610 --> 00:59:57.110 align:middle line:84%
So in here, what I
want to write is,

00:59:57.110 --> 00:59:59.550 align:middle line:84%
what are the expected
payoffs if player 1

00:59:59.550 --> 01:00:02.970 align:middle line:84%
uses this mixed strategy
and player 2 plays B?

01:00:02.970 --> 01:00:04.450 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, plays A.

01:00:04.450 --> 01:00:06.590 align:middle line:84%
And then, over here,
what am I going to write?

01:00:06.590 --> 01:00:09.530 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to write the payoffs--
if player 1 plays this mixed

01:00:09.530 --> 01:00:14.810 align:middle line:84%
strategy and player 2 plays
B. And it turns out, this--

01:00:14.810 --> 01:00:18.010 align:middle line:90%
well, let's go through it.

01:00:18.010 --> 01:00:22.170 align:middle line:84%
If player 2 plays and player
1 plays A with probability 1/2

01:00:22.170 --> 01:00:26.850 align:middle line:84%
and B with probability 1/2,
then what is the payoff of--

01:00:26.850 --> 01:00:29.050 align:middle line:84%
what are the payoffs going
to be for the players?

01:00:29.050 --> 01:00:33.090 align:middle line:84%
Well, player 1 is going to get
negative 1 with probability 1/2

01:00:33.090 --> 01:00:37.330 align:middle line:84%
and 1 with probability 1/2,
which just gives them 0.

01:00:37.330 --> 01:00:40.490 align:middle line:84%
And player 2 is also going
to get 1 with probability 1/2

01:00:40.490 --> 01:00:42.190 align:middle line:84%
and negative 1 with
probability 1/2.

01:00:42.190 --> 01:00:44.378 align:middle line:84%
So we just get 0, and
if we go to this side,

01:00:44.378 --> 01:00:46.170 align:middle line:84%
we're actually just
going to get 0's again.

01:00:46.170 --> 01:00:49.370 align:middle line:84%
And mathematically, the way
we form this row of the matrix

01:00:49.370 --> 01:00:52.770 align:middle line:84%
is we just take this
row, multiply it by half,

01:00:52.770 --> 01:00:55.990 align:middle line:84%
take this row, multiply it by
half, and add them together.

01:00:55.990 --> 01:00:59.890 align:middle line:84%
And when we do that, we
exactly get 0, 0, 0, 0.

01:00:59.890 --> 01:01:02.830 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, the
key thing about this

01:01:02.830 --> 01:01:06.070 align:middle line:84%
is that these
numbers are the same.

01:01:06.070 --> 01:01:13.230 align:middle line:84%
So what this tells me is if
player 1 plays 1/2 A plus 1/2 B,

01:01:13.230 --> 01:01:17.710 align:middle line:84%
then player 2 gets a payoff of 0
if they play A and a payoff of 0

01:01:17.710 --> 01:01:21.510 align:middle line:84%
if they play B. And that means
that player 2 is indifferent

01:01:21.510 --> 01:01:24.390 align:middle line:84%
between A and B, and in fact,
A and B are both pure best

01:01:24.390 --> 01:01:25.350 align:middle line:90%
responses.

01:01:25.350 --> 01:01:27.190 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm indifferent
between A and B,

01:01:27.190 --> 01:01:30.790 align:middle line:84%
then I'm also willing to mix
over A and B. And therefore,

01:01:30.790 --> 01:01:34.870 align:middle line:84%
it's a best response for
player 2 to mix 1/2, 1/2.

01:01:34.870 --> 01:01:39.190 align:middle line:84%
So technically, what I've shown
is if player 1 is mixing 1/2,

01:01:39.190 --> 01:01:42.888 align:middle line:84%
1/2, it's a best response
for player 2 to mix 1/2, 1/2.

01:01:42.888 --> 01:01:44.930 align:middle line:84%
So technically, if I want
to finish the argument,

01:01:44.930 --> 01:01:46.710 align:middle line:84%
I need to show the
other direction,

01:01:46.710 --> 01:01:49.910 align:middle line:84%
that if player 2 mixes 1/2,
1/2, then it's a best response

01:01:49.910 --> 01:01:52.390 align:middle line:84%
for player 1 to mix 1/2, 1/2,
but it's going to be entirely

01:01:52.390 --> 01:01:52.930 align:middle line:90%
symmetric.

01:01:52.930 --> 01:01:55.290 align:middle line:84%
I could add another
column here and go 0, 0,

01:01:55.290 --> 01:01:58.745 align:middle line:90%
but we can check that--

01:01:58.745 --> 01:01:59.870 align:middle line:90%
I'll just put a check mark.

01:01:59.870 --> 01:02:00.690 align:middle line:90%
We checked it.

01:02:00.690 --> 01:02:01.550 align:middle line:90%
It's an equilibrium.

01:02:01.550 --> 01:02:03.870 align:middle line:84%
Don't do that on your homework,
but that's all we need.

01:02:03.870 --> 01:02:08.530 align:middle line:90%


01:02:08.530 --> 01:02:11.930 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's return
to the Boston game.

01:02:11.930 --> 01:02:13.370 align:middle line:90%
I don't know if we have it.

01:02:13.370 --> 01:02:14.992 align:middle line:90%
I may have to write it again.

01:02:14.992 --> 01:02:15.950 align:middle line:90%
You can write it again.

01:02:15.950 --> 01:02:17.490 align:middle line:90%
That's OK.

01:02:17.490 --> 01:02:19.710 align:middle line:90%
So now let's go to the BOS game.

01:02:19.710 --> 01:02:21.290 align:middle line:90%
This is a bit trickier.

01:02:21.290 --> 01:02:29.750 align:middle line:84%
And we have Celtics, Red Sox,
Celtics, Red Sox 2, 1, 1, 2, 0,

01:02:29.750 --> 01:02:32.210 align:middle line:90%
0, 0, 0.

01:02:32.210 --> 01:02:35.290 align:middle line:84%
So this game, we already
found some pure equilibria.

01:02:35.290 --> 01:02:36.450 align:middle line:90%
We found two of them.

01:02:36.450 --> 01:02:39.130 align:middle line:84%
We said there's one equilibrium
where we both play C,

01:02:39.130 --> 01:02:41.810 align:middle line:84%
and there's one equilibrium
where we both play R.

01:02:41.810 --> 01:02:44.990 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out, there's also
a mixed-strategy equilibrium.

01:02:44.990 --> 01:02:50.730 align:middle line:84%
So here, what we'd like to do
is find a mixed-strategy Nash

01:02:50.730 --> 01:02:52.390 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium of this game.

01:02:52.390 --> 01:02:55.290 align:middle line:90%


01:02:55.290 --> 01:02:58.720 align:middle line:84%
Now, of course, we could say the
pure-strategy Nash equilibria

01:02:58.720 --> 01:03:00.920 align:middle line:84%
we already found are
technically mixed-strategy Nash

01:03:00.920 --> 01:03:04.420 align:middle line:84%
equilibria where people's mixing
probabilities are just 1 and 0.

01:03:04.420 --> 01:03:07.280 align:middle line:84%
But what I mean is something
that's actually mixed.

01:03:07.280 --> 01:03:10.340 align:middle line:84%
So what I really mean is a
mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium

01:03:10.340 --> 01:03:12.660 align:middle line:84%
that's not a pure-strategy
Nash equilibrium.

01:03:12.660 --> 01:03:15.720 align:middle line:90%


01:03:15.720 --> 01:03:18.617 align:middle line:84%
OK, well, I think the
first step is let's

01:03:18.617 --> 01:03:19.700 align:middle line:90%
just write some notations.

01:03:19.700 --> 01:03:20.980 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to look
for an equilibrium.

01:03:20.980 --> 01:03:22.480 align:middle line:84%
We're going to look
for some mixing.

01:03:22.480 --> 01:03:26.160 align:middle line:84%
So let's look for an
equilibrium of the form-- well,

01:03:26.160 --> 01:03:31.120 align:middle line:84%
player 1 is going to put,
say, probability P1 on C.

01:03:31.120 --> 01:03:35.360 align:middle line:84%
And let's say player 2 is going
to put probability P2 on C.

01:03:35.360 --> 01:03:38.840 align:middle line:84%
So our goal is to find a Nash
equilibrium of the form player

01:03:38.840 --> 01:03:44.440 align:middle line:84%
1 plays P1 times C plus
1 minus P1 times R.

01:03:44.440 --> 01:03:47.120 align:middle line:84%
So what this means is,
with probability P1,

01:03:47.120 --> 01:03:49.480 align:middle line:84%
player 1 goes to
the Celtics game,

01:03:49.480 --> 01:03:52.640 align:middle line:84%
and with probability
1 minus P1, player 1

01:03:52.640 --> 01:03:54.520 align:middle line:90%
goes to the Red Sox game.

01:03:54.520 --> 01:03:55.920 align:middle line:84%
And then, similarly
for player 2,

01:03:55.920 --> 01:03:57.920 align:middle line:84%
but we have potentially
a different number here.

01:03:57.920 --> 01:04:06.386 align:middle line:90%


01:04:06.386 --> 01:04:09.700 align:middle line:84%
And we want to find an
equilibrium of this form

01:04:09.700 --> 01:04:12.820 align:middle line:84%
where maybe what we'd like is
we want it to actually involve

01:04:12.820 --> 01:04:13.320 align:middle line:90%
mixing.

01:04:13.320 --> 01:04:15.740 align:middle line:90%
So let's say P1--

01:04:15.740 --> 01:04:19.500 align:middle line:84%
0 is less than P1 is less
than 1, and 0 is less than P2

01:04:19.500 --> 01:04:20.360 align:middle line:90%
is less than 1.

01:04:20.360 --> 01:04:21.000 align:middle line:90%
This is what we want.

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:23.292 align:middle line:84%
We want something where each
player is actually mixing.

01:04:23.292 --> 01:04:27.535 align:middle line:84%
They're not just playing one
strategy with probability 1.

01:04:27.535 --> 01:04:29.160 align:middle line:84%
So I guess the trick
is to think, well,

01:04:29.160 --> 01:04:30.660 align:middle line:90%
what must be true here?

01:04:30.660 --> 01:04:36.460 align:middle line:84%
So if we want this to be a
mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium,

01:04:36.460 --> 01:04:41.220 align:middle line:84%
it must be that player 1 is
willing to mix between C and R,

01:04:41.220 --> 01:04:42.910 align:middle line:84%
given the way that
player 2 is playing.

01:04:42.910 --> 01:04:45.160 align:middle line:84%
And based on what we said
before, what does that mean?

01:04:45.160 --> 01:04:49.240 align:middle line:84%
What must be true if player 1 is
willing to mix between C and R?

01:04:49.240 --> 01:04:52.120 align:middle line:90%


01:04:52.120 --> 01:04:52.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:04:52.620 --> 01:04:56.000 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Player 1
[INAUDIBLE] C, R [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:56.000 --> 01:04:58.480 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Exactly, and
then the other direction.

01:04:58.480 --> 01:05:00.600 align:middle line:84%
Player 2 must also be
indifferent between C

01:05:00.600 --> 01:05:02.705 align:middle line:90%
and R, given player 1 strategy.

01:05:02.705 --> 01:05:04.080 align:middle line:84%
So the way that
we as the analyst

01:05:04.080 --> 01:05:06.280 align:middle line:84%
often solve for an
equilibrium like this is we

01:05:06.280 --> 01:05:07.920 align:middle line:84%
look for mixing
probabilities that

01:05:07.920 --> 01:05:09.140 align:middle line:90%
make the players indifferent.

01:05:09.140 --> 01:05:12.200 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at this,
there's two steps.

01:05:12.200 --> 01:05:20.540 align:middle line:84%
So the mixture P1 must
make player 2 indifferent.

01:05:20.540 --> 01:05:31.500 align:middle line:90%


01:05:31.500 --> 01:05:32.500 align:middle line:90%
And this is a key point.

01:05:32.500 --> 01:05:35.280 align:middle line:84%
You might think I made a typo
here, but I didn't actually.

01:05:35.280 --> 01:05:37.760 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that the
probability with which player

01:05:37.760 --> 01:05:42.880 align:middle line:84%
1 mixes is what determines
whether player 2 is indifferent

01:05:42.880 --> 01:05:47.320 align:middle line:84%
because player 1's mixing
probabilities in equilibrium

01:05:47.320 --> 01:05:49.800 align:middle line:84%
determine the beliefs
that player 2 has.

01:05:49.800 --> 01:05:52.140 align:middle line:84%
So whether player
2 wants to play

01:05:52.140 --> 01:05:56.140 align:middle line:84%
C or R depends on their
beliefs about player 1

01:05:56.140 --> 01:05:57.820 align:middle line:84%
and the frequency
with which player

01:05:57.820 --> 01:06:00.260 align:middle line:84%
1 plays C or R, so it's,
actually, player 1's

01:06:00.260 --> 01:06:03.740 align:middle line:84%
mixing probability that is
responsible for determining

01:06:03.740 --> 01:06:05.500 align:middle line:90%
whether player 2 is indifferent.

01:06:05.500 --> 01:06:07.820 align:middle line:84%
And conversely, it's
player 2's mixing

01:06:07.820 --> 01:06:11.540 align:middle line:84%
probability that determines
whether player 1 is indifferent.

01:06:11.540 --> 01:06:14.140 align:middle line:84%
So when you try to solve
for these equilibrium,

01:06:14.140 --> 01:06:15.695 align:middle line:90%
it all looks nice.

01:06:15.695 --> 01:06:17.820 align:middle line:84%
We have two indifference
conditions and two numbers

01:06:17.820 --> 01:06:18.680 align:middle line:90%
to solve for.

01:06:18.680 --> 01:06:21.260 align:middle line:84%
Mathematically, it's pretty
easy, but the thing to remember

01:06:21.260 --> 01:06:24.780 align:middle line:84%
is that player 2's indifference
condition is actually,

01:06:24.780 --> 01:06:26.860 align:middle line:84%
in this case, going to
be determined by P1,

01:06:26.860 --> 01:06:29.180 align:middle line:84%
and player 1's going
to be determined by P2.

01:06:29.180 --> 01:06:31.160 align:middle line:84%
So player 2's
indifferent condition,

01:06:31.160 --> 01:06:35.460 align:middle line:84%
we need to make sure that player
2 gets the same payoff from C

01:06:35.460 --> 01:06:40.180 align:middle line:84%
as from R. So let's
look at player 2.

01:06:40.180 --> 01:06:46.340 align:middle line:84%
If player 2 plays C, well,
they get a payoff of 1

01:06:46.340 --> 01:06:50.800 align:middle line:84%
if player 1 plays C and a
payoff of 0 if player 1 plays R.

01:06:50.800 --> 01:06:54.440 align:middle line:84%
So what is their
expected payoff?

01:06:54.440 --> 01:06:57.240 align:middle line:84%
Their expected
payoff from playing C

01:06:57.240 --> 01:07:03.640 align:middle line:84%
is going to be P1 times 1
plus 1 minus P1 times 0.

01:07:03.640 --> 01:07:05.560 align:middle line:84%
But the 0 disappears,
and we just

01:07:05.560 --> 01:07:08.320 align:middle line:90%
get P1 in the left-hand side.

01:07:08.320 --> 01:07:13.800 align:middle line:84%
So here, P1 is the utility,
the expected utility

01:07:13.800 --> 01:07:16.140 align:middle line:90%
for player 2 from playing C.

01:07:16.140 --> 01:07:19.200 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll put a
C here and an R here

01:07:19.200 --> 01:07:24.280 align:middle line:84%
just to keep track of
what we're comparing.

01:07:24.280 --> 01:07:25.960 align:middle line:90%
And this makes sense.

01:07:25.960 --> 01:07:28.640 align:middle line:84%
If I'm player 2, and I
go to the Celtics game,

01:07:28.640 --> 01:07:30.820 align:middle line:84%
I know that if my opponent
goes to the Red Sox game,

01:07:30.820 --> 01:07:32.160 align:middle line:90%
I get a payoff of 0.

01:07:32.160 --> 01:07:34.080 align:middle line:84%
So my only utility
comes from if they also

01:07:34.080 --> 01:07:37.100 align:middle line:84%
go to the Celtics game, that
happens with probability P1.

01:07:37.100 --> 01:07:39.160 align:middle line:84%
And if it does happen,
I get a payoff of 1

01:07:39.160 --> 01:07:40.827 align:middle line:84%
because I like
the Red Sox more--

01:07:40.827 --> 01:07:42.660 align:middle line:84%
I only get a payoff of
one from the Celtics.

01:07:42.660 --> 01:07:43.743 align:middle line:90%
What about the other case?

01:07:43.743 --> 01:07:45.040 align:middle line:90%
If we have the R--

01:07:45.040 --> 01:07:47.460 align:middle line:84%
if I play r, what is
my payoff going to be?

01:07:47.460 --> 01:07:50.540 align:middle line:90%


01:07:50.540 --> 01:07:51.233 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:07:51.233 --> 01:07:52.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
2 times 1 minus R.

01:07:52.900 --> 01:07:54.000 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly, right.

01:07:54.000 --> 01:07:57.580 align:middle line:90%


01:07:57.580 --> 01:08:01.220 align:middle line:84%
So here, again, I get no payoff
if they go the other way.

01:08:01.220 --> 01:08:04.580 align:middle line:84%
1 minus P1 is the probability
that my friend also

01:08:04.580 --> 01:08:05.662 align:middle line:90%
goes to the Red Sox game.

01:08:05.662 --> 01:08:08.120 align:middle line:84%
But now there's a 2 here because
I really like the Red Sox.

01:08:08.120 --> 01:08:09.870 align:middle line:84%
Or if they go to the
Red Sox game with me,

01:08:09.870 --> 01:08:12.540 align:middle line:84%
I get a payoff of
2 rather than 1.

01:08:12.540 --> 01:08:14.660 align:middle line:84%
And now let's do the
same thing for player 1.

01:08:14.660 --> 01:08:15.820 align:middle line:90%
I'm player 1.

01:08:15.820 --> 01:08:19.500 align:middle line:84%
If I play C, given that
my opponent is playing

01:08:19.500 --> 01:08:21.939 align:middle line:84%
C with probability
P2, what is my payoff

01:08:21.939 --> 01:08:23.120 align:middle line:90%
going to be as player 1?

01:08:23.120 --> 01:08:26.700 align:middle line:90%


01:08:26.700 --> 01:08:28.859 align:middle line:90%
Well, it's going to be 2.

01:08:28.859 --> 01:08:33.779 align:middle line:84%
I get 2 with probability P2 and
0 with probability 1 minus P2,

01:08:33.779 --> 01:08:35.439 align:middle line:90%
so I just get 2 P2.

01:08:35.439 --> 01:08:38.859 align:middle line:90%


01:08:38.859 --> 01:08:43.620 align:middle line:84%
And then if I play R, again,
0 with probability P2, 1

01:08:43.620 --> 01:08:47.460 align:middle line:84%
with probability 1 minus
P2, so I get 1 minus P2.

01:08:47.460 --> 01:08:54.160 align:middle line:90%


01:08:54.160 --> 01:08:58.279 align:middle line:84%
And we can just do
some algebra here.

01:08:58.279 --> 01:09:05.960 align:middle line:84%
And if we pull this over, we're
going to get P2 equals 1/3.

01:09:05.960 --> 01:09:08.020 align:middle line:90%
And then P1 equals 2/3.

01:09:08.020 --> 01:09:13.788 align:middle line:90%


01:09:13.788 --> 01:09:15.580 align:middle line:84%
So I want to caution
here against, I think,

01:09:15.580 --> 01:09:17.660 align:middle line:84%
a common
misinterpretation of this.

01:09:17.660 --> 01:09:22.040 align:middle line:90%


01:09:22.040 --> 01:09:24.880 align:middle line:84%
What a lot of people say is
they say, OK, this makes sense.

01:09:24.880 --> 01:09:28.300 align:middle line:84%
Player 1 goes to the
Celtics game more often.

01:09:28.300 --> 01:09:31.520 align:middle line:84%
P1 is 2/3, more than 1/2,
because player 1 likes

01:09:31.520 --> 01:09:34.000 align:middle line:90%
the Celtics.

01:09:34.000 --> 01:09:38.120 align:middle line:84%
And player 2 goes to
the Red Sox game more.

01:09:38.120 --> 01:09:40.819 align:middle line:84%
Player 2 goes to Celtics game
only with probability 1/3,

01:09:40.819 --> 01:09:43.319 align:middle line:84%
which means they go to the Red
Sox game with probability 2/3

01:09:43.319 --> 01:09:47.340 align:middle line:84%
because player 2 likes
the Red Sox more.

01:09:47.340 --> 01:09:49.892 align:middle line:90%
Is that right?

01:09:49.892 --> 01:09:51.600 align:middle line:84%
Do you see a problem
with that reasoning?

01:09:51.600 --> 01:09:52.359 align:middle line:90%
I don't know, what do you think?

01:09:52.359 --> 01:09:53.526 align:middle line:90%
Does that seem right to you?

01:09:53.526 --> 01:10:00.060 align:middle line:90%


01:10:00.060 --> 01:10:02.340 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out that
the reason, if we really

01:10:02.340 --> 01:10:04.500 align:middle line:84%
look through the
equilibrium, the reason

01:10:04.500 --> 01:10:08.620 align:middle line:84%
that player 1 goes
to the Celtics a lot

01:10:08.620 --> 01:10:11.140 align:middle line:84%
is not because they
like the Celtics more

01:10:11.140 --> 01:10:14.300 align:middle line:84%
but because their opponent
likes the Celtics less.

01:10:14.300 --> 01:10:16.860 align:middle line:84%
Player 1's mixing
probabilities have

01:10:16.860 --> 01:10:19.380 align:middle line:90%
to make player 2 indifferent.

01:10:19.380 --> 01:10:22.580 align:middle line:84%
And because player 2 doesn't
like the Celtics very much,

01:10:22.580 --> 01:10:25.220 align:middle line:84%
they're only willing to
go to the Celtics game

01:10:25.220 --> 01:10:28.180 align:middle line:84%
if they believe that player 1 is
very often going to the Celtics

01:10:28.180 --> 01:10:29.340 align:middle line:90%
game.

01:10:29.340 --> 01:10:31.040 align:middle line:84%
And let's do the
reverse reasoning.

01:10:31.040 --> 01:10:34.060 align:middle line:84%
Why does player two go to
the Red Sox game a lot?

01:10:34.060 --> 01:10:36.860 align:middle line:84%
It's true that player 2
likes the Red Sox a lot,

01:10:36.860 --> 01:10:38.788 align:middle line:84%
but the real reason
for this equilibrium

01:10:38.788 --> 01:10:40.580 align:middle line:84%
is that they have to
go to the Red Sox game

01:10:40.580 --> 01:10:44.120 align:middle line:84%
a lot to make player 1 willing
to go to the Red Sox game

01:10:44.120 --> 01:10:45.842 align:middle line:84%
because player 1
prefers the Celtics,

01:10:45.842 --> 01:10:47.800 align:middle line:84%
so they're only going to
go to the Red Sox game

01:10:47.800 --> 01:10:49.800 align:middle line:84%
if they think it's
more likely than not

01:10:49.800 --> 01:10:51.920 align:middle line:84%
that player 2 goes
to the Red Sox game.

01:10:51.920 --> 01:10:54.480 align:middle line:84%
So I think that's
just a key point

01:10:54.480 --> 01:11:00.415 align:middle line:84%
here about what's driving the
structure of this equilibrium.

01:11:00.415 --> 01:11:02.040 align:middle line:84%
And I think that
should make you wonder

01:11:02.040 --> 01:11:05.480 align:middle line:84%
a bit because I shouldn't
care about making

01:11:05.480 --> 01:11:07.000 align:middle line:90%
my opponent indifferent.

01:11:07.000 --> 01:11:15.092 align:middle line:84%
So I think an issue,
maybe a question,

01:11:15.092 --> 01:11:22.020 align:middle line:84%
is if I'm player 1 here, given
the way my opponent is playing,

01:11:22.020 --> 01:11:24.600 align:middle line:84%
I am indifferent
between C and R.

01:11:24.600 --> 01:11:27.480 align:middle line:84%
So why should I mix with exactly
the right probability that

01:11:27.480 --> 01:11:29.960 align:middle line:90%
makes my opponent indifferent?

01:11:29.960 --> 01:11:32.520 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't really seem like,
if I'm indifferent, I mean,

01:11:32.520 --> 01:11:35.140 align:middle line:84%
why go through the difficulty
of doing all the randomization?

01:11:35.140 --> 01:11:37.540 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's a question
we'll just leave for now.

01:11:37.540 --> 01:11:39.500 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk a bit more throughout
the course about this,

01:11:39.500 --> 01:11:41.292 align:middle line:84%
but I think it's
something to keep in mind,

01:11:41.292 --> 01:11:44.360 align:middle line:84%
that it's true, I'm willing to
mix, but why do I actually mix?

01:11:44.360 --> 01:11:49.660 align:middle line:90%


01:11:49.660 --> 01:11:51.818 align:middle line:90%
We do see it in practice.

01:11:51.818 --> 01:11:52.360 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:11:52.360 --> 01:11:53.120 align:middle line:90%
I will open it up.

01:11:53.120 --> 01:11:54.078 align:middle line:90%
Are there any thoughts?

01:11:54.078 --> 01:11:57.462 align:middle line:84%
I mean, why do you think
poker players actually do mix?

01:11:57.462 --> 01:11:59.420 align:middle line:84%
They're indifferent, they
should be indifferent

01:11:59.420 --> 01:12:01.120 align:middle line:84%
if everyone else is
mixing just right.

01:12:01.120 --> 01:12:02.400 align:middle line:90%
Why do they mix?

01:12:02.400 --> 01:12:06.660 align:middle line:90%


01:12:06.660 --> 01:12:08.780 align:middle line:84%
Why would you mix if
you took penalties?

01:12:08.780 --> 01:12:10.400 align:middle line:84%
What would go wrong
if you didn't mix?

01:12:10.400 --> 01:12:13.900 align:middle line:90%


01:12:13.900 --> 01:12:15.293 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

01:12:15.293 --> 01:12:17.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They would know
what you were going to do,

01:12:17.460 --> 01:12:19.800 align:middle line:84%
and they would update their
strategy based on that.

01:12:19.800 --> 01:12:21.300 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right,
so often, the story

01:12:21.300 --> 01:12:23.217 align:middle line:84%
we tell about mixed
strategies is often really

01:12:23.217 --> 01:12:25.837 align:middle line:84%
a story about a dynamic process
where the game is repeated.

01:12:25.837 --> 01:12:28.420 align:middle line:84%
And for this reason, we might
think that a mixed-strategy Nash

01:12:28.420 --> 01:12:31.580 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium is a more compelling
prediction if the game is

01:12:31.580 --> 01:12:32.980 align:middle line:90%
repeated over time.

01:12:32.980 --> 01:12:34.860 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to
have a reputation

01:12:34.860 --> 01:12:37.500 align:middle line:84%
as someone who always shoots
left because then your opponents

01:12:37.500 --> 01:12:38.543 align:middle line:90%
would update on that.

01:12:38.543 --> 01:12:40.460 align:middle line:84%
Now, then we have to
really think about, well,

01:12:40.460 --> 01:12:42.580 align:middle line:84%
what does it mean to develop
a reputation for something?

01:12:42.580 --> 01:12:44.240 align:middle line:84%
And that takes us
down a whole new path

01:12:44.240 --> 01:12:46.490 align:middle line:84%
that people have tried to
formalize and think through.

01:12:46.490 --> 01:12:49.160 align:middle line:84%
It's not clear
exactly what it means.

01:12:49.160 --> 01:12:52.160 align:middle line:90%
So that's one story.

01:12:52.160 --> 01:12:53.800 align:middle line:84%
I guess, another
story I would say

01:12:53.800 --> 01:12:58.280 align:middle line:84%
is sometimes mixing
is not literal mixing,

01:12:58.280 --> 01:13:01.080 align:middle line:84%
but what mixing means is
you're making a decision based

01:13:01.080 --> 01:13:05.120 align:middle line:84%
on something that the other
person isn't aware of.

01:13:05.120 --> 01:13:07.120 align:middle line:90%
So it may be that--

01:13:07.120 --> 01:13:10.560 align:middle line:90%


01:13:10.560 --> 01:13:12.580 align:middle line:84%
let's take, I don't know,
how someone dresses.

01:13:12.580 --> 01:13:13.680 align:middle line:84%
You may think, oh,
if someone just

01:13:13.680 --> 01:13:15.840 align:middle line:84%
randomizes the way they
dress, but really, they

01:13:15.840 --> 01:13:17.600 align:middle line:84%
wear certain colors
on certain weather

01:13:17.600 --> 01:13:19.500 align:middle line:84%
because that makes
them feel happy.

01:13:19.500 --> 01:13:21.800 align:middle line:84%
But to you, not knowing
their careful algorithm

01:13:21.800 --> 01:13:22.760 align:middle line:84%
for how they choose
their clothes

01:13:22.760 --> 01:13:25.280 align:middle line:84%
as a function of the weather,
it just appears random to you.

01:13:25.280 --> 01:13:26.960 align:middle line:84%
Or similarly, maybe
a penalty kicker,

01:13:26.960 --> 01:13:29.868 align:middle line:84%
when they come to the
spot, feels a direction.

01:13:29.868 --> 01:13:31.660 align:middle line:84%
One of their feet feels
a little different,

01:13:31.660 --> 01:13:33.160 align:middle line:84%
they just feel a
direction to go to,

01:13:33.160 --> 01:13:36.080 align:middle line:84%
and then they make a
deterministic choice about which

01:13:36.080 --> 01:13:38.740 align:middle line:84%
direction to shoot, but
because you, as the opponent,

01:13:38.740 --> 01:13:41.160 align:middle line:84%
can't see that signal that
they're basing their shot on,

01:13:41.160 --> 01:13:42.380 align:middle line:90%
it appears random to you.

01:13:42.380 --> 01:13:44.640 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes we literally
observe randomization,

01:13:44.640 --> 01:13:47.460 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes behaviors
appear random because they're

01:13:47.460 --> 01:13:49.900 align:middle line:84%
based upon things that we
can't actually observe.

01:13:49.900 --> 01:13:52.660 align:middle line:84%
So just one thing
to keep in mind.

01:13:52.660 --> 01:13:57.580 align:middle line:90%
And I think that's good.

01:13:57.580 --> 01:14:01.300 align:middle line:90%
Let me finish with one last--

01:14:01.300 --> 01:14:02.320 align:middle line:90%
two last things.

01:14:02.320 --> 01:14:05.260 align:middle line:90%


01:14:05.260 --> 01:14:10.340 align:middle line:84%
So notice that, in the game
that we looked at here,

01:14:10.340 --> 01:14:14.140 align:middle line:84%
this game did not have a
pure-strategy Nash equilibrium.

01:14:14.140 --> 01:14:16.940 align:middle line:84%
But when we moved to
mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium,

01:14:16.940 --> 01:14:19.158 align:middle line:84%
it did have a mixed
strategy Nash equilibrium.

01:14:19.158 --> 01:14:20.700 align:middle line:84%
And the question
is, could we come up

01:14:20.700 --> 01:14:23.300 align:middle line:84%
with some really weird game
where there's no equilibrium.

01:14:23.300 --> 01:14:25.383 align:middle line:84%
That maybe wouldn't be so
very good for our theory

01:14:25.383 --> 01:14:27.550 align:middle line:84%
because then we wouldn't
have a prediction about how

01:14:27.550 --> 01:14:28.060 align:middle line:90%
people play.

01:14:28.060 --> 01:14:30.260 align:middle line:84%
And fortunately, it turns
out-- and this is really

01:14:30.260 --> 01:14:35.730 align:middle line:84%
Nash's key result in 1948-- that
that's never going to happen.

01:14:35.730 --> 01:14:40.970 align:middle line:84%
Every game, within reason, has a
mixed strategy Nash equilibrium.

01:14:40.970 --> 01:14:47.230 align:middle line:84%
So more precisely, let me say
every finite strategic-form

01:14:47.230 --> 01:14:47.730 align:middle line:90%
game--

01:14:47.730 --> 01:14:52.650 align:middle line:90%


01:14:52.650 --> 01:14:55.150 align:middle line:84%
would be a little more precise,
I'll say-- has at least one.

01:14:55.150 --> 01:15:12.250 align:middle line:90%


01:15:12.250 --> 01:15:13.210 align:middle line:90%
So at least one.

01:15:13.210 --> 01:15:14.440 align:middle line:90%
It might have only one.

01:15:14.440 --> 01:15:15.190 align:middle line:90%
It might have two.

01:15:15.190 --> 01:15:16.023 align:middle line:90%
It might have three.

01:15:16.023 --> 01:15:18.650 align:middle line:84%
It may have infinitely many,
but it has at least one.

01:15:18.650 --> 01:15:21.530 align:middle line:84%
And when I say mixed strategy,
that includes pure strategy

01:15:21.530 --> 01:15:22.550 align:middle line:90%
as a special case.

01:15:22.550 --> 01:15:25.137 align:middle line:84%
So I might say mixed
or pure, but remember,

01:15:25.137 --> 01:15:26.970 align:middle line:84%
we're going to think
of a pure-strategy Nash

01:15:26.970 --> 01:15:31.290 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium as a special
case of a mixed-strategy Nash

01:15:31.290 --> 01:15:33.670 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium where
the players mix

01:15:33.670 --> 01:15:36.150 align:middle line:90%
with degenerate probabilities.

01:15:36.150 --> 01:15:38.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is not something we're
going to prove in the course.

01:15:38.750 --> 01:15:41.630 align:middle line:84%
This is actually quite a
deep result. So remember,

01:15:41.630 --> 01:15:43.870 align:middle line:84%
a couple of weeks
ago, last week, we

01:15:43.870 --> 01:15:46.450 align:middle line:84%
talked about this
dominance/best response duality,

01:15:46.450 --> 01:15:47.730 align:middle line:90%
and I also didn't prove that.

01:15:47.730 --> 01:15:49.010 align:middle line:90%
That is somewhat deep.

01:15:49.010 --> 01:15:49.850 align:middle line:90%
This is much deeper.

01:15:49.850 --> 01:15:52.510 align:middle line:84%
So this is maybe the
mathematically deepest result

01:15:52.510 --> 01:15:53.510 align:middle line:90%
in the course.

01:15:53.510 --> 01:15:56.370 align:middle line:84%
And it uses something called
a fixed-point theorem.

01:15:56.370 --> 01:16:04.693 align:middle line:90%


01:16:04.693 --> 01:16:06.110 align:middle line:84%
And if you want
to google it, it's

01:16:06.110 --> 01:16:09.090 align:middle line:84%
either sometimes attributed
to Brouwer or Kakutani.

01:16:09.090 --> 01:16:11.870 align:middle line:90%


01:16:11.870 --> 01:16:15.250 align:middle line:84%
And these are, actually, quite
deep mathematical results.

01:16:15.250 --> 01:16:17.310 align:middle line:84%
So one of the
mathematical results

01:16:17.310 --> 01:16:19.730 align:middle line:84%
was actually proven,
I think, in 1944.

01:16:19.730 --> 01:16:22.170 align:middle line:84%
And then Nash proved
this result in 1948.

01:16:22.170 --> 01:16:24.390 align:middle line:84%
So it was really using
cutting-edge math

01:16:24.390 --> 01:16:26.550 align:middle line:84%
to prove this new result.
Maybe not so cutting edge

01:16:26.550 --> 01:16:30.110 align:middle line:84%
anymore in 1944, but at the
time, the gap between the math

01:16:30.110 --> 01:16:33.730 align:middle line:84%
frontier and the econ frontier
was actually very, very close.

01:16:33.730 --> 01:16:36.410 align:middle line:84%
If you want to
see the proof, you

01:16:36.410 --> 01:16:38.030 align:middle line:90%
can look in the lecture notes.

01:16:38.030 --> 01:16:41.610 align:middle line:84%
The lecture notes give a proof
building upon these theorems.

01:16:41.610 --> 01:16:43.690 align:middle line:84%
The real depth is proving
these theorems, which

01:16:43.690 --> 01:16:44.610 align:middle line:90%
we're not going to talk about.

01:16:44.610 --> 01:16:45.690 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to
talk about this proof

01:16:45.690 --> 01:16:47.910 align:middle line:84%
either, but just want
you to keep that in mind.

01:16:47.910 --> 01:16:49.990 align:middle line:84%
And before we go, we
have three more minutes.

01:16:49.990 --> 01:16:52.810 align:middle line:84%
So let me just conclude with
a few interpretations of Nash

01:16:52.810 --> 01:16:55.770 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

01:16:55.770 --> 01:16:58.650 align:middle line:84%
I think we talk about
Nash equilibrium

01:16:58.650 --> 01:17:00.690 align:middle line:84%
as the crowning
achievement of game theory.

01:17:00.690 --> 01:17:03.050 align:middle line:84%
But over the last
seven years, people

01:17:03.050 --> 01:17:05.210 align:middle line:84%
have always been searching
for justifications

01:17:05.210 --> 01:17:06.230 align:middle line:90%
of Nash equilibrium.

01:17:06.230 --> 01:17:08.850 align:middle line:84%
And I would actually argue
that Nash equilibrium does not

01:17:08.850 --> 01:17:11.430 align:middle line:84%
have as compelling of a
justification as, say,

01:17:11.430 --> 01:17:12.462 align:middle line:90%
rationalizability does.

01:17:12.462 --> 01:17:14.170 align:middle line:84%
Of course, it makes
stronger assumptions,

01:17:14.170 --> 01:17:16.503 align:middle line:84%
and it gets sharper predictions,
but whether we actually

01:17:16.503 --> 01:17:18.750 align:middle line:84%
find it compelling, I think,
is not totally obvious.

01:17:18.750 --> 01:17:21.430 align:middle line:84%
Again, on exams, if I ask you
to solve for a Nash equilibrium,

01:17:21.430 --> 01:17:21.950 align:middle line:90%
you have to solve it.

01:17:21.950 --> 01:17:24.283 align:middle line:84%
You can't just say, I don't
believe in Nash equilibrium,

01:17:24.283 --> 01:17:27.130 align:middle line:84%
but I do think it's helpful to
think about how compelling this

01:17:27.130 --> 01:17:27.630 align:middle line:90%
is.

01:17:27.630 --> 01:17:31.110 align:middle line:84%
So I want to present a few
interpretations of this.

01:17:31.110 --> 01:17:36.870 align:middle line:84%
So one interpretation is
about a stable convention.

01:17:36.870 --> 01:17:39.850 align:middle line:84%
So the idea of Nash equilibrium
is really about stability.

01:17:39.850 --> 01:17:41.910 align:middle line:84%
It's that given the way
people are behaving,

01:17:41.910 --> 01:17:43.430 align:middle line:90%
no one wants to deviate.

01:17:43.430 --> 01:17:46.310 align:middle line:84%
So a great, I think, example
of this is the side of the road

01:17:46.310 --> 01:17:47.600 align:middle line:90%
that we drive on.

01:17:47.600 --> 01:17:49.350 align:middle line:84%
Why do we all drive
on the right in the US

01:17:49.350 --> 01:17:50.725 align:middle line:84%
and drive on the
left in England?

01:17:50.725 --> 01:17:53.410 align:middle line:84%
You might say, oh, you get a
ticket if you drove on the left,

01:17:53.410 --> 01:17:54.990 align:middle line:84%
but that's not
really the reason.

01:17:54.990 --> 01:17:56.850 align:middle line:84%
It's really because you're
afraid you'd get hit by a car.

01:17:56.850 --> 01:17:58.950 align:middle line:84%
So if everyone drives on
the right, it's best for you

01:17:58.950 --> 01:17:59.970 align:middle line:90%
to drive on the right.

01:17:59.970 --> 01:18:01.510 align:middle line:84%
If everyone drives on the
left, it's best for you

01:18:01.510 --> 01:18:02.410 align:middle line:90%
to drive on the left.

01:18:02.410 --> 01:18:04.910 align:middle line:84%
These are both
stable conventions,

01:18:04.910 --> 01:18:06.700 align:middle line:84%
and we don't really
need to ticket people

01:18:06.700 --> 01:18:08.450 align:middle line:84%
for driving on the
wrong side of the road.

01:18:08.450 --> 01:18:10.825 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we do, but that's not
really what motivates them,

01:18:10.825 --> 01:18:13.550 align:middle line:84%
I think, to drive on the
correct side of the road.

01:18:13.550 --> 01:18:16.610 align:middle line:84%
Another is you can think of it
as a self-enforcing agreement.

01:18:16.610 --> 01:18:21.430 align:middle line:90%


01:18:21.430 --> 01:18:23.710 align:middle line:84%
Again, self-enforcing
because no one

01:18:23.710 --> 01:18:25.770 align:middle line:84%
wants to unilaterally
deviate from the agreement.

01:18:25.770 --> 01:18:27.312 align:middle line:84%
And I think this
explains the success

01:18:27.312 --> 01:18:29.470 align:middle line:84%
of a lot of multilateral
international agreements.

01:18:29.470 --> 01:18:31.730 align:middle line:84%
When countries sign
treaties, there's

01:18:31.730 --> 01:18:34.590 align:middle line:84%
no court that can adjudicate
a treaty between countries,

01:18:34.590 --> 01:18:37.290 align:middle line:90%
or generally, there's not.

01:18:37.290 --> 01:18:39.240 align:middle line:84%
So why do people
follow the treaty?

01:18:39.240 --> 01:18:41.490 align:middle line:84%
Well, often when treaties
are set up in the right way,

01:18:41.490 --> 01:18:43.790 align:middle line:84%
people don't have an incentive
to unilaterally deviate.

01:18:43.790 --> 01:18:45.050 align:middle line:84%
And even though there's
not some court that

01:18:45.050 --> 01:18:47.170 align:middle line:84%
would throw a country
in prison, they often

01:18:47.170 --> 01:18:49.890 align:middle line:84%
are still motivated to
follow these agreements.

01:18:49.890 --> 01:18:54.650 align:middle line:84%
And then another interpretation
is as a steady state.

01:18:54.650 --> 01:18:56.970 align:middle line:84%
And this gets to a
discussion of penalty kicks.

01:18:56.970 --> 01:18:58.790 align:middle line:84%
If you were to
always shoot right,

01:18:58.790 --> 01:19:00.370 align:middle line:84%
the goalies would start
always guessing that you

01:19:00.370 --> 01:19:01.270 align:middle line:90%
were going to shoot right.

01:19:01.270 --> 01:19:03.870 align:middle line:84%
So then you might start shooting
left, and then, over time,

01:19:03.870 --> 01:19:06.370 align:middle line:84%
we think that you might
converge to the steady state.

01:19:06.370 --> 01:19:09.730 align:middle line:84%
Whether there's actually
a real justification

01:19:09.730 --> 01:19:12.110 align:middle line:84%
for dynamics converging is
actually a bit more subtle.

01:19:12.110 --> 01:19:15.475 align:middle line:84%
But the point is, if you are in
a Nash equilibrium, it's steady,

01:19:15.475 --> 01:19:17.350 align:middle line:84%
and there's no reason
to move away from that.

01:19:17.350 --> 01:19:19.030 align:middle line:84%
And that's the final
interpretation,

01:19:19.030 --> 01:19:20.070 align:middle line:90%
and I'll leave it there.

01:19:20.070 --> 01:19:22.360 align:middle line:90%
So I'll see everyone next week.

01:19:22.360 --> 01:19:26.000 align:middle line:90%